window.CHG_BANK=[{"ar":"Art. 90 Introduction","step":"2017->2020","f":"Scope of the NEC: shore power for boats in marinas... what was added in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: 90.2(A) listed four covered installations and shore power was not among them. 2020 NEC: new 90.2(A)(5) adds installations supplying shore power to ships and watercraft in marinas and boatyards, including the monitoring of leakage current.","r":"NEC 90.2(A)(5) (2020); 90.2(A) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which installation is specifically listed in 90.2(A) as covered by the Code?","o":["Installations underground in mines","Installations of railway power and signaling","Installations in ships and watercraft other than floating buildings","Installations supplying shore power to watercraft in marinas and boatyards, including leakage current monitoring"],"a":3,"e":"2020 90.2(A)(5) is new. The other choices are the kind of installations that 90.2(B) says the Code does NOT cover (ships and watercraft other than floating buildings, underground mines, railway power and signaling). Find it in the book at 90.2(A), the list of covered installations.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 90 Introduction","step":"2017->2020","f":"Scope of the NEC: electric vehicles exporting power to a building... what changed in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: 90.2(A) did not list vehicle power export. 2020 NEC: new 90.2(A)(6) covers installations used to export power from electric vehicles to premises wiring, or that allow bidirectional current flow.","r":"NEC 90.2(A)(6) (2020); 90.2(A) (2017)","q":"Which new item was added to the scope of the NEC at 90.2(A)(6) in the 2020 edition?","o":["Installations used to export power from electric vehicles to premises wiring or that allow bidirectional current flow","Installations of wireless charging pads only","Installations of public utility EV fast-charging stations on utility property","Electrical equipment inside the electric vehicle itself"],"a":0,"e":"2020 added 90.2(A)(6) so that vehicle-to-home and other bidirectional EV power transfer is within the Code. The wiring and equipment inside the vehicle is not covered. Look at 90.2(A) in the 2020 book.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions","step":"2017->2020","f":"Where do the hazardous (classified) location definitions live in the 2020 NEC?","b":"2017 NEC: Article 100 had Part I (1000 V or less) and Part II (over 1000 V), and hazardous location definitions were in the Chapter 5 articles. 2020 NEC: the Article 100 scope adds a Part III, Hazardous (Classified) Locations, holding those definitions, and notes that some definitions also appear in the XXX.2 section of other articles.","r":"NEC Article 100 Scope, Part III (2020); Article 100 (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, in which Article 100 part are definitions applying to hazardous (classified) locations located?","o":["They remain only in Article 500","Part I, 1000 Volts, Nominal, or Less only","Part III, Hazardous (Classified) Locations","Part II, Over 1000 Volts, Nominal only"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 edition created Article 100 Part III so the hazardous location terms are in one place, with no technical change intended. In the 2017 edition they were spread through the Chapter 5 .2 sections.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions","step":"2017->2020","f":"New 2020 definition tied to ceiling fan boxes... what is a Habitable Room?","b":"2017 NEC: no Article 100 definition of Habitable Room. 2020 NEC: new definition of Habitable Room, a room in a building intended for living, sleeping, eating or cooking; it excludes bathrooms, toilet rooms, closets, hallways, storage or utility spaces and similar areas. It is used in the fan box rule of 314.27(C).","r":"NEC Art. 100 Habitable Room (2020); 314.27(C) (2020)","q":"Which section of the 2020 NEC makes direct use of the new Article 100 term Habitable Room?","o":["210.52(A), general wall receptacle spacing","250.104(A), bonding of metal water piping","230.67, surge protection for services","314.27(C), outlet boxes at ceiling-suspended (paddle) fan locations in dwellings"],"a":3,"e":"The new term supports 314.27(C), which applies to ceiling outlet boxes in habitable rooms of dwelling units. The other sections do not use the term. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Habitable Room; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions","step":"2017->2020","f":"New Article 100 definition... Reconditioned equipment in the 2020 NEC?","b":"2017 NEC: no general definition of Reconditioned in Article 100. 2020 NEC: new Reconditioned definition (equipment restored to functional operation by inspection, repair, replacement of parts, testing and similar) with an informational note listing related terms such as rebuilt, refurbished and remanufactured.","r":"NEC Art. 100 Reconditioned (2020)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, which informational note terms are listed with the new Article 100 definition of Reconditioned?","o":["Rebuilt, refurbished, and remanufactured","Listed, labeled, and identified","Qualified, approved, and accepted","Field labeled, field evaluated, and field marked"],"a":0,"e":"2020 added the Reconditioned definition because new rules (210.15, 240.88, 408.8, 406.3, 410.7 and others) limit reconditioned equipment. The other groups are unrelated Article 100 terms.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions","step":"2017->2020","f":"New 2020 definition for microgrid operation... Island Mode?","b":"2017 NEC: no Article 100 definition of Island Mode. 2020 NEC: Island Mode is added to Article 100 and describes standalone or isolated microgrid operation, which supports Articles 705, 710 and 712.","r":"NEC Art. 100 Island Mode (2020)","q":"A new Article 100 definition in the 2020 NEC, Island Mode, relates most closely to which situation?","o":["A microgrid or power system operating isolated from the primary source","A panelboard fed by two main breakers","A building supplied by two separate utility services","An ungrounded delta system with a ground detector"],"a":0,"e":"Island Mode was added in 2020 and describes isolated or standalone microgrid operation. It is used in Chapter 7 for microgrids and stand-alone systems. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Island Mode; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions","step":"2017->2020","f":"New Article 100 definition... Dormitory Unit?","b":"2017 NEC: no Article 100 definition of Dormitory Unit. 2020 NEC: new definition describing a building or space where group sleeping accommodations are provided for more than 16 persons who are not members of the same family, under joint occupancy and single management.","r":"NEC Art. 100 Dormitory Unit (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a Dormitory Unit as defined in Article 100 provides group sleeping accommodations for more than how many unrelated persons?","o":["16","8","24","4"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 definition ties to NFPA 101 style occupancies with more than 16 unrelated sleepers. The term is used in rules such as 210.12 and 406.12. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Dormitory Unit; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions","step":"2017->2020","f":"New 2020 Article 100 definition used for GFCI... Laundry Area?","b":"2017 NEC: Laundry Area was not defined in Article 100. 2020 NEC: new definition of Laundry Area as an area containing or designed to contain a laundry tray, clothes washer, and/or clothes dryer.","r":"NEC Art. 100 Laundry Area (2020)","q":"Which equipment is part of the Article 100 definition of Laundry Area in the 2020 NEC?","o":["Only a clothes dryer","Only a clothes washer and its receptacle","A water heater and a furnace","A laundry tray, clothes washer, and/or clothes dryer"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC defined Laundry Area because 210.8(A) and 210.8(B) rely on the term for GFCI locations. Look it up in Article 100. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Laundry Area; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions","step":"2017->2020","f":"Where is the definition of Supply-Side Bonding Jumper in 2020 vs 2017?","b":"2017 NEC: the definition of Bonding Jumper, Supply-Side was in 250.2. 2020 NEC: it was relocated to Article 100 as part of the move of widely used terms into Article 100 (no technical change).","r":"NEC Art. 100 (2020); 250.2 (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, where is the definition of Bonding Jumper, Supply-Side found?","o":["250.102(C)","Article 100","230.2","250.2 only"],"a":1,"e":"In 2017 the term was defined in 250.2. The 2020 edition moved it to Article 100 because it is used in several articles. This is a relocation, not a new rule. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Bonding Jumper, Supply-Side; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","step":"2017->2020","f":"Neat and workmanlike installation of cables and conductors... where is it in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: 'neat and workmanlike' mechanical execution of cables was repeated in 725.24, 760.24, 770.24, 800.24, 820.24, 830.24 and 840.24. 2020 NEC: the general rule is relocated to new 110.12(C), Cables and Conductors, with the supporting informational notes.","r":"NEC 110.12(C) (2020); 725.24, 760.24, 770.24, 800.24 (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which section now contains the general workmanship requirements for exposed cables and conductors that previously appeared in the individual Chapter 7 and 8 articles?","o":["300.4(A)","110.12(C)","300.11(A)","110.3(B)"],"a":1,"e":"The 2017 edition repeated the language in each xxx.24 section of the low-voltage and communications articles. 2020 consolidated it in 110.12(C). Check 110.12 in the 2020 book.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","step":"2017->2020","f":"Terminal torque: calibrated torque tool vs approved means... what changed?","b":"2017 NEC: where a numeric torque value is given, a calibrated torque tool was required (unless the manufacturer allowed another method). 2020 NEC: the manufacturer's torque value must be achieved using an approved means, such as a torque tool or a torque-indicating fastener, and three informational notes were added.","r":"NEC 110.14(D) (2020); 110.14(D) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 110.14(D), where a torque value is specified for a terminal, how must the required torque be achieved?","o":["By tightening until snug and then a quarter turn","Only with a calibrated torque screwdriver","By an approved means, such as a torque tool or a torque-indicating fastener","Torque values are advisory and need not be verified"],"a":2,"e":"The 2017 text named a calibrated torque tool; 2020 broadened it to an approved means and added informational notes. Do not confuse with the older practice of tightening by feel.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","step":"2017->2020","f":"Marking of reconditioned equipment: what must be removed or added?","b":"2017 NEC: reconditioned equipment had to be marked as reconditioned. 2020 NEC: the original listing mark must also be removed from reconditioned equipment, in addition to the 'reconditioned' marking.","r":"NEC 110.21(A)(2) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, what must be done to the original listing mark on equipment that has been reconditioned?","o":["It may remain if the equipment passes a test","It must be moved to the back of the enclosure","It must be left in place and covered with a label","The original listing mark must be removed"],"a":3,"e":"110.21(A)(2) in 2020 pairs the 'reconditioned' marking with removal of the original listing mark so the product is not mistaken for new listed equipment. This ties to the new Reconditioned definition.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","step":"2017->2020","f":"Disconnect identification: what extra information is required outside one- and two-family dwellings?","b":"2017 NEC: each disconnecting means had to be legibly marked to show its purpose (unless evident). 2020 NEC: in other than one- and two-family dwellings, the marking must also identify the circuit source that supplies the disconnecting means, and the marking must be durable.","r":"NEC 110.22(A) (2020); 110.22(A) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a disconnecting means in a commercial building must be marked to show its purpose and also to identify what?","o":["The date the disconnect was installed","The available fault current at the load","The circuit source that supplies the disconnecting means","The name of the licensed electrician"],"a":2,"e":"The added source-of-supply marking in 110.22(A) is for other than one- and two-family dwellings. Available fault current marking is a different rule (110.24 and 408.6). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 110.22(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","step":"2017->2020","f":"Connection of premises wiring to a grounded supply system... what wording changed?","b":"2017 NEC: 200.3 addressed 'interior' wiring systems being connected to a supply with a grounded conductor. 2020 NEC: the rule applies to all premises wiring (indoors and outdoors), and an exception recognizes listed utility-interactive inverters that connect without a grounded conductor.","r":"NEC 200.3 (2020); 200.3 (2017)","q":"The 2020 NEC revised 200.3 so that the rule on connecting to a supply system's grounded conductor applies to what?","o":["Only services over 600 volts","All premises wiring systems, not only interior wiring","Only separately derived systems","Only interior wiring systems"],"a":1,"e":"The 2017 text referred to interior wiring; 2020 uses premises wiring so outdoor installations are included. Look at 200.3 in the 2020 book. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 200.3; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","step":"2017->2020","f":"White terminal identification for grounded conductors... what changed?","b":"2017 NEC: terminals for a grounded conductor were required to be identified by a substantially white color. 2020 NEC: the white identification is limited to terminals for polarized connections, and silver is recognized as an acceptable color.","r":"NEC 200.9 (2020); 200.9 (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a terminal to which a grounded conductor is connected on a polarized device may be identified by which color?","o":["Only blue","Only yellow","Only green","Substantially white or silver"],"a":3,"e":"2020 clarified that the requirement is for polarized connections and recognized silver. Green is for equipment grounding terminals (250.126). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 200.9; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"GFCI for dwelling units... what voltage and ampere range of receptacles in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: GFCI in the 210.8(A) dwelling locations applied to 125-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles. 2020 NEC: it applies to all 125-volt through 250-volt receptacles on single-phase branch circuits rated 150 volts or less to ground, with no ampere limit (so 240 V dryer, range and shop receptacles in listed locations are included).","r":"NEC 210.8(A) (2020); 210.8(A) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which receptacle in a dwelling unit garage requires GFCI protection under 210.8(A)?","o":["A 240-volt, single-phase receptacle on a 150-volts-or-less-to-ground circuit, such as one for a welder","Only 125-volt receptacles within 6 ft of a sink","None; garage GFCI was removed for 250-volt equipment","Only 125-volt, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles"],"a":0,"e":"The 2017 NEC limited 210.8(A) to 125-volt 15/20-ampere receptacles. The 2020 NEC extended it to 125 through 250 volts, single-phase, 150 volts or less to ground. A 120/240-volt single-phase system qualifies.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"Dwelling basements and GFCI... finished vs unfinished?","b":"2017 NEC: GFCI applied to unfinished portions of basements not intended as habitable rooms. 2020 NEC: GFCI applies to all basement receptacles (finished and unfinished). An exception remains for a receptacle supplying only a permanently installed fire or burglar alarm system.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(5) (2020); 210.8(A)(5) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which dwelling unit basement receptacle requires GFCI protection?","o":["Only receptacles in unfinished storage areas of the basement","Only receptacles within 6 ft of a basement sink","A 125-volt receptacle in a finished basement family room","None if the basement has a bedroom"],"a":2,"e":"The 2017 text used 'unfinished' basement areas. 2020 simply says basements, removing the judgment about finished space. The alarm-only receptacle is the exception. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.8(A)(5); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"New dwelling GFCI location in 2020: damp and wet locations?","b":"2017 NEC: 210.8(A) listed ten locations and had no general item for indoor damp or wet locations in dwellings. 2020 NEC: new 210.8(A)(11) requires GFCI protection for receptacles in indoor damp and wet locations.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(11) (2020)","q":"Which new item was added to the list of dwelling unit locations in 210.8(A) in the 2020 NEC?","o":["Indoor damp and wet locations","Bedroom closets","Living room walls above 6 ft","Attic spaces over 10 ft high"],"a":0,"e":"210.8(A)(11) is new for 2020 and reaches places like mud rooms. Bedrooms, living rooms and attics are not GFCI locations under 210.8(A). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.8(A)(11); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"GFCI outside dwellings (210.8(B)): receptacle voltage and current range?","b":"2017 NEC: 210.8(B) covered 125-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles in the listed non-dwelling locations. 2020 NEC: it covers 125- through 250-volt receptacles; single-phase up to 50 A and three-phase up to 100 A, on circuits 150 volts or less to ground.","r":"NEC 210.8(B) (2020); 210.8(B) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 210.8(B), which receptacle in a listed non-dwelling location requires GFCI protection?","o":["A three-phase receptacle rated 100 amperes or less on a circuit of 150 volts or less to ground","Only receptacles rated 15 amperes","Only single-phase 125-volt, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles","Three-phase receptacles are exempt from GFCI in all cases"],"a":0,"e":"2020 broadened 210.8(B) to higher ratings: single-phase 50 A or less and three-phase 100 A or less, 150 V to ground or less. In 2017 it was limited to 125 V, 15 and 20 A.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"New GFCI locations outside dwellings in 2020: laundry areas?","b":"2017 NEC: laundry areas in non-dwelling occupancies were not listed in 210.8(B). 2020 NEC: new 210.8(B)(11) requires GFCI for receptacles in laundry areas.","r":"NEC 210.8(B)(11) (2020)","q":"Which location was added to 210.8(B) (other than dwelling units) in the 2020 NEC?","o":["Offices","Corridors","Conference rooms","Laundry areas"],"a":3,"e":"2020 added laundry areas to 210.8(B) (item 11) alongside areas within 6 ft of a bathtub or shower (item 12). Offices and corridors are not listed. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.8(B)(11); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"New GFCI location outside dwellings: near tubs and showers?","b":"2017 NEC: no separate item for receptacles near bathtubs and shower stalls outside dwellings (bathrooms were already covered). 2020 NEC: new 210.8(B)(12) requires GFCI for receptacles within 6 ft of the outside edge of a bathtub or shower stall.","r":"NEC 210.8(B)(12) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a receptacle in a non-dwelling occupancy that is within what distance of the edge of a bathtub or shower stall requires GFCI protection under 210.8(B)(12)?","o":["3 ft","6 ft","10 ft","20 ft"],"a":1,"e":"210.8(B)(12) is new in 2020 and uses the same 6 ft measure as other GFCI sink and tub provisions. Pool receptacles use 20 ft in 680.22. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.8(B)(12); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"Dishwasher GFCI moved... from where to where in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: 210.8(D) required GFCI for dishwasher outlets in dwelling units. 2020 NEC: 210.8(D) is 'Specific Appliances' and points to 422.5; dishwasher GFCI is in 422.5(A)(7) and now covers all dishwashers, not only those in dwelling units.","r":"NEC 210.8(D), 422.5(A)(7) (2020); 210.8(D) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, where is the GFCI requirement for dishwashers found?","o":["680.5","422.16(B)(2) only","210.8(A)(7) only","422.5(A)(7), referenced by 210.8(D)"],"a":3,"e":"2020 moved the dishwasher rule to Article 422 and expanded it beyond dwelling units. 210.8(D) now just cross-references 422.5. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.8(D); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"GFCI for HVAC service receptacles... new 2020 requirement?","b":"2017 NEC: 210.8(E) was crawl space lighting; no GFCI rule for service receptacles. 2020 NEC: new 210.8(E) requires GFCI protection for the receptacles required by 210.63 for servicing heating, air-conditioning and refrigeration equipment.","r":"NEC 210.8(E), 210.63 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which receptacle requires GFCI protection under 210.8(E)?","o":["Only receptacles in dwelling bathrooms","Receptacles in the main electrical room only","The receptacle for a dedicated range circuit","The 125-volt receptacle required by 210.63 for servicing HVAC equipment on a rooftop"],"a":3,"e":"210.8(E) is new in 2020 and ties GFCI protection to the service receptacle required by 210.63. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.8(E); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"Outdoor outlets at dwellings... new GFCI rule for equipment like AC units?","b":"2017 NEC: GFCI for dwelling outdoor receptacles only (210.8(A)(3)). 2020 NEC: new 210.8(F) requires GFCI for all outdoor outlets at dwelling units, not just receptacles, supplied by single-phase branch circuits rated 150 volts or less to ground and 50 amperes or less (for example hardwired HVAC equipment). Lighting outlets are excepted.","r":"NEC 210.8(F) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a 240-volt, 30-ampere hardwired air conditioner condensing unit outdoors at a one-family dwelling must be supplied by what?","o":["A standard circuit breaker without GFCI, since it is not a receptacle","A GFCI-protected branch circuit","An AFCI-protected circuit only","A circuit of at least 60 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"210.8(F) was added in 2020 for outdoor outlets (not only receptacles). The circuit must be single-phase, 150 V or less to ground, 50 A or less. Outdoor lighting outlets are excepted.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"AFCI expanded to guest rooms and nursing home patient rooms?","b":"2017 NEC: AFCI protection was required for dwelling units and dormitory units. 2020 NEC: new 210.12(C) extends AFCI protection to guest rooms and guest suites and to patient sleeping rooms of nursing homes and limited-care facilities.","r":"NEC 210.12(C) (2020); 210.12 (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, AFCI protection is newly required for 15- and 20-ampere, 120-volt branch circuits in which location?","o":["Warehouse offices","Hospital operating rooms","Parking structures","Patient sleeping rooms of nursing homes and limited-care facilities"],"a":3,"e":"2020 added 210.12(C). Operating rooms, warehouses and parking structures are not on the list. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.12(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"AFCI for branch circuit extensions... what changed in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: AFCI was required when branch circuits were modified, replaced or extended in dwelling and dormitory units, using a combination-type AFCI at the origin or an outlet-type AFCI at the first outlet. 2020 NEC: the rule also covers guest rooms and guest suites, the first option allows any means in 210.12(A)(1) through (A)(6), and in the exception conductors inside an enclosure do not count toward the 6 ft limit and adding a splicing device does not trigger AFCI.","r":"NEC 210.12(D) (2020); 210.12(C) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, the branch circuit extension rule of 210.12(D) now also applies to which space?","o":["Garages of commercial buildings","Guest rooms and guest suites","Outdoor receptacles","Hospital patient rooms only"],"a":1,"e":"2020 added guest rooms and guest suites to the extension and modification rule. The 6 ft exception measures only conductors outside enclosures. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.12(D); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"Can GFCI and AFCI devices be reconditioned? New 2020 section?","b":"2017 NEC: no section addressing reconditioning of GFCI, AFCI or ground-fault protection equipment. 2020 NEC: new 210.15 prohibits reconditioning of ground-fault circuit interrupters, arc-fault circuit interrupters and ground-fault protection of equipment.","r":"NEC 210.15 (2020)","q":"Which statement about reconditioning is correct under the 2020 NEC 210.15?","o":["Only AFCI devices may not be reconditioned","Reconditioned GFCIs are allowed in dwelling units only","GFCI, AFCI and ground-fault protection of equipment devices may not be reconditioned","GFCI devices may be reconditioned if retested"],"a":2,"e":"210.15 is new in 2020. It bars reconditioning of protective devices where failure could cause shock or fire. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.15; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 220 Branch-Circuit","step":"2017->2020","f":"Non-dwelling lighting unit loads changed in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: Table 220.12 unit loads were largely unchanged for decades (for example office 3.5 VA/sq ft). 2020 NEC: unit loads were reduced for most occupancies (and increased for a few such as warehouses and gymnasiums), aligned with energy codes.","r":"NEC Table 220.12 (2020); Table 220.12 (2017)","q":"How did the 2020 NEC change the general lighting unit loads in Table 220.12 for most non-dwelling occupancies compared with 2017?","o":["They were reduced for most occupancies","They were removed in favor of the energy code only","They were increased for every occupancy","They were left unchanged"],"a":0,"e":"2020 lowered most values based on lighting energy data while raising a few. The 2017 values were the long-standing numbers. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 220.12 / Table 220.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2017->2020","f":"Splicing and tapping service conductors... what new listing requirement?","b":"2017 NEC: 230.46 allowed service conductors to be spliced or tapped per 110.14, 300.5(E), 300.13 and 300.15. 2020 NEC: devices used to splice or tap service conductors (including power distribution blocks) must be listed and marked for line-side use; marking enforcement for pressure connectors and splice devices starts January 1, 2023.","r":"NEC 230.46 (2020); 230.46 (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, power distribution blocks used to tap service conductors on the line side of the service disconnect must be marked how?","o":["No marking is required","Listed for load-side use only","Suitable for use on the line side of the service equipment","Rated for use only with copper"],"a":2,"e":"2020 expanded 230.46 to require listed devices marked for line-side use. Load-side-only blocks are not permitted for this use. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.46; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2017->2020","f":"Barriers in service equipment... where did the rule move?","b":"2017 NEC: barriers to prevent contact with uninsulated line-side service busbars and terminals were in the panelboard, switchboard and switchgear articles (408.3(A)(2)). 2020 NEC: moved to new 230.62(C) and applies to all service equipment.","r":"NEC 230.62(C) (2020); 408.3(A)(2) (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, where is the barrier requirement for uninsulated energized service busbars and terminals found?","o":["408.3(A)(2)","250.92(A)","110.27(A)","230.62(C)"],"a":3,"e":"The 2017 rule sat in Article 408 and applied to panelboards, switchboards and switchgear. The 2020 rule is in Article 230 and covers all service equipment. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.62(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2017->2020","f":"Surge protection for dwelling services... new in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: no surge protection requirement for dwelling unit services. 2020 NEC: new 230.67 requires a Type 1 or Type 2 surge protective device for all services supplying dwelling units, integral to the service equipment or immediately adjacent to it.","r":"NEC 230.67 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, all services supplying dwelling units must be provided with what?","o":["A ground-fault protection device","A Type 1 or Type 2 surge protective device","An AFCI main breaker","A Type 3 SPD at each receptacle"],"a":1,"e":"230.67 is new for 2020. The SPD is Type 1 or Type 2, installed integral to or adjacent to the service equipment. Type 3 point-of-use SPDs do not satisfy it. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.67; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2017->2020","f":"Surge protection at the service... when is it required to be added?","b":"2017 NEC: no SPD rule for dwelling services. 2020 NEC: 230.67 requires the SPD on new services and when service equipment is replaced; an exception allows the SPD at the next level of distribution equipment downstream toward the load.","r":"NEC 230.67 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, when an existing dwelling unit's service equipment is replaced, what applies?","o":["No SPD is needed unless a new service is installed","A Type 1 or Type 2 SPD must be installed per 230.67","An SPD is needed only for services over 200 amperes","A Type 4 component assembly is required"],"a":1,"e":"230.67 applies to replacement of service equipment as well as new services. The exception permits the SPD downstream at the next distribution equipment. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.67; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2017->2020","f":"How many service disconnects, and where? Six in one panelboard still allowed?","b":"2017 NEC: up to six switches or circuit breakers could be grouped in one enclosure (for example a panelboard with six mains) as the service disconnecting means. 2020 NEC: 230.71(A) is one disconnect per service unless 230.71(B) applies; (B) allows two to six disconnects only in separate enclosures, separate panelboards, or separate vertical sections or compartments of switchboards or switchgear.","r":"NEC 230.71(A), (B) (2020); 230.71 (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which arrangement does NOT comply with 230.71 for a service with six disconnects?","o":["Six disconnects in separate compartments of switchgear","Six disconnects in separate vertical sections of a switchboard","Six switches in six separate enclosures","All six circuit breakers mounted in a single panelboard enclosure"],"a":3,"e":"2020 stopped permitting up to six service disconnects in one panelboard enclosure; they must be separated per 230.71(B). Also 408.36 Exception for six mains was deleted. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.71; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2017->2020","f":"Devices not counted as service disconnects in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: 230.71 counted all disconnects when applying the six-disconnect limit. 2020 NEC: power monitoring equipment, surge protective devices, ground-fault protection control circuits and power-operable disconnect controls are not counted as service disconnects; handle-tie provisions for single-pole devices were deleted.","r":"NEC 230.71(A) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 230.71, which is NOT counted toward the number of service disconnects?","o":["A switch in a meter-main combination serving as a service disconnect","A circuit breaker serving as a service disconnect","A surge protective device","A fused switch serving as a service disconnect"],"a":2,"e":"230.71(A) in 2020 excludes power monitoring, SPD, GFP control circuits and power-operable disconnect functions. Actual service disconnecting devices are counted. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.71; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2017->2020","f":"Emergency disconnect for dwellings... new in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: no requirement for an outdoor emergency disconnect at dwellings. 2020 NEC: new 230.85 requires, for one- and two-family dwellings, an emergency disconnect at a readily accessible outdoor location; it may be a service disconnect, a meter disconnect (230.82(3)), or another listed disconnect on the supply side, marked per 110.21(B) and grouped with the disconnects.","r":"NEC 230.85 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a one-family dwelling's service must have an emergency disconnect located where?","o":["Inside the garage next to the panel","At a readily accessible location outside the building","In the basement near the meter","At the utility pole only"],"a":1,"e":"230.85 requires an outdoor, readily accessible emergency disconnect for 1- and 2-family dwellings to let first responders de-energize the structure. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.85; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2017->2020","f":"Emergency disconnect marking in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: no emergency disconnect marking. 2020 NEC: 230.85 requires marking: EMERGENCY DISCONNECT, SERVICE DISCONNECT for a service disconnect used as the emergency disconnect, or EMERGENCY DISCONNECT, NOT SERVICE EQUIPMENT for other types, in the form required by 110.21(B).","r":"NEC 230.85 (2020); 110.21(B)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, an outdoor emergency disconnect that is not the service disconnect must be marked how?","o":["EMERGENCY DISCONNECT, NOT SERVICE EQUIPMENT","FIRE DEPARTMENT CONNECTION","EMERGENCY SHUTDOWN, SERVICE DISCONNECT","MAIN DISCONNECT, SERVICE EQUIPMENT"],"a":0,"e":"230.85 distinguishes between a service-disconnect emergency disconnect and a non-service one by the marking text. This rule did not exist in 2017. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.85; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","step":"2017->2020","f":"Restricted access adjustable-trip breakers: ways to restrict access... what was added?","b":"2017 NEC: three methods listed for restricting access to adjustable trip settings (sealable covers, bolted doors, locked doors accessible only to qualified persons). 2020 NEC: password protection is added as a fourth method.","r":"NEC 240.6(C) (2020); 240.6(C) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 240.6(C), which is an accepted method of restricting access to adjustable trip settings of a circuit breaker?","o":["Using a keyed receptacle on the load side","Password protection","Posting a warning label near the breaker","Mounting the breaker above 6 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Password protection was added to 240.6(C) in 2020 along with removable and sealable covers, bolted enclosure doors and locked doors. A label or mounting height does not restrict access.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","step":"2017->2020","f":"Arc energy reduction for fuses (1200 A and up): what did 2020 add?","b":"2017 NEC: 240.67 required arc energy reduction for fuses rated or settable at 1200 A or higher but had no on-site testing rule. 2020 NEC: documentation that the method operates below the available arcing current is required, current-limiting electronically actuated fuses are added as a method, and 240.67(C) requires performance testing when first installed.","r":"NEC 240.67(A), (C) (2020); 240.67 (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 240.67(C), arc energy reduction for 1200 A or higher fuses requires what?","o":["A coordination study of all downstream fuses","Performance testing of the method when first installed","Only a label stating the maintenance mode","Nothing beyond the 2017 requirements"],"a":1,"e":"Performance testing was added in 2020 for fuses in 240.67(C) and for breakers in 240.87(C). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 240.67(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","step":"2017->2020","f":"Arc energy reduction for breakers: which method is NOT allowed?","b":"2017 NEC: 240.87 listed methods to reduce clearing time but did not expressly address temporarily raising the instantaneous setting. 2020 NEC: temporary adjustment of the instantaneous trip setting is not permitted as an arc energy reduction method; documentation that the method is set below the available arcing current and performance testing (C) were added.","r":"NEC 240.87(B), (C) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 240.87, which is NOT an acceptable arc energy reduction method?","o":["Differential relaying","Zone-selective interlocking","Temporarily adjusting the instantaneous trip setting","An energy-reducing maintenance switch"],"a":2,"e":"2020 clarified that temporary instantaneous adjustment is not a permitted method. ZSI, differential relaying and an energy-reducing maintenance switch are permitted methods.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","step":"2017->2020","f":"Reconditioned breakers: new section in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: no section specific to reconditioned circuit breakers. 2020 NEC: new 240.88 allows reconditioning of certain breakers (low-voltage power, medium and high-voltage) but molded-case circuit breakers may not be reconditioned; reconditioned equipment must be listed as reconditioned and the original listing mark removed.","r":"NEC 240.88 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 240.88, which circuit breaker may NOT be reconditioned?","o":["A high-voltage circuit breaker","A molded-case circuit breaker","A low-voltage power circuit breaker","A medium-voltage circuit breaker"],"a":1,"e":"240.88 is new in 2020 and prohibits reconditioning molded-case breakers. Larger power breakers may be reconditioned when listed as reconditioned. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 240.88; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 242 Overvoltage Protection","step":"2017->2020","f":"Surge protection articles 280 and 285... what replaced them?","b":"2017 NEC: surge arresters over 1000 V were in Article 280 and surge-protective devices (1000 V or less) were in Article 285. 2020 NEC: Articles 280 and 285 are deleted and combined into new Article 242, Overvoltage Protection.","r":"NEC Article 242 (2020); Articles 280, 285 (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, surge-protective devices rated 1000 volts or less are covered in which article?","o":["Article 242","Article 245","Article 285","Article 280"],"a":0,"e":"The 2017 NEC used Article 285 for SPDs and Article 280 for surge arresters. 2020 combined them into Article 242. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Art. 242; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","step":"2017->2020","f":"Rebar and grounding electrodes: can rebar connect electrodes together?","b":"2017 NEC: text could be read as allowing the rebar of a footing or foundation to be used to interconnect grounding electrodes. 2020 NEC: rebar is prohibited as a conductor to interconnect grounding electrodes.","r":"NEC 250.53 (2020); 250.68(C)(3) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which is prohibited?","o":["Using a concrete-encased electrode as a grounding electrode","Using a ground rod electrode","Using rebar as the conductor to interconnect grounding electrodes","Extending rebar to a connection point if tied or continuous and protected from earth contact"],"a":2,"e":"250.53 and 250.68(C)(3) in 2020 say rebar may not interconnect electrodes. A concrete-encased electrode is still allowed. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 250.53; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","step":"2017->2020","f":"Grounding electrode conductor protection in PVC... what is specified?","b":"2017 NEC: PVC conduit was allowed for protection of grounding electrode conductors without a schedule being named. 2020 NEC: where a GEC is protected by PVC conduit, Schedule 80 PVC is specified.","r":"NEC 250.64(B) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, what type of PVC is specified to protect a grounding electrode conductor from physical damage?","o":["Schedule 20 PVC","Schedule 40 PVC","Any thin-wall PVC","Schedule 80 PVC"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC calls for Schedule 80 PVC in 250.64(B) where PVC protection is used. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 250.64(B); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","step":"2017->2020","f":"Rebar of a concrete-encased electrode as a connection point?","b":"2017 NEC: rebar extension rules were unclear. 2020 NEC: 250.68(C)(3) permits a rebar extension from a concrete-encased electrode as a connection point if the rebar is continuous or tied together and protected from contact with the earth (corrosion protection), but it cannot be used to interconnect electrodes.","r":"NEC 250.68(C)(3) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 250.68(C)(3), a rebar extension from a concrete-encased electrode may serve as a connection point when it is protected from what?","o":["Contact with copper conductors","Exposure to sunlight","Contact with the earth","Contact with concrete"],"a":2,"e":"The rebar extension must be protected from contact with earth (corrosion protection) under 250.68(C)(3)(a)-(c). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 250.68(C)(3); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","step":"2017->2020","f":"Bonding jumper to metal water piping: is there a maximum size?","b":"2017 NEC: the bonding jumper to metal water piping was sized from Table 250.102(C)(1) with no stated maximum. 2020 NEC: the bonding jumper need not be larger than 3/0 AWG copper or 250 kcmil aluminum or copper-clad aluminum.","r":"NEC 250.104(A)(1) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 250.104(A)(1), a bonding jumper to metal water piping is never required to be larger than what?","o":["500 kcmil copper","3/0 AWG copper or 250 kcmil aluminum or copper-clad aluminum","4/0 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper"],"a":1,"e":"2020 capped the water-pipe bonding jumper at 3/0 copper (250 kcmil aluminum). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 250.104(A)(1); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","step":"2017->2020","f":"Increasing EGC size when ungrounded conductors are upsized: adjustment factors?","b":"2017 NEC: when ungrounded conductors were increased in size, wire-type EGCs had to be increased proportionately. 2020 NEC: no EGC increase is required where the phase conductors are increased only because of ambient temperature correction or conductor adjustment factors, and an exception lets a qualified person size the EGC to ensure an effective ground-fault path.","r":"NEC 250.122(B) (2020); 250.122(B) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, when phase conductors are increased in size solely because of adjustment factors for more than three current-carrying conductors, the EGC must be:","o":["Increased to the same size as the phase conductors","Increased proportionately","Sized per Table 250.122 without increasing it proportionately","Replaced by a bonding jumper"],"a":2,"e":"250.122(B) in 2020 excludes increases made for adjustment or correction factors. The 2017 rule applied to any upsizing. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 250.122(B); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","step":"2017->2020","f":"Clean surfaces: grounding only, or bonding too?","b":"2017 NEC: 250.12 required nonconductive coatings to be removed to ensure continuity for grounding connections. 2020 NEC: clean surfaces are required for bonding as well as grounding connections.","r":"NEC 250.12 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 250.12, nonconductive coatings such as paint must be removed from surfaces for which connections?","o":["Bonding connections only","Both grounding and bonding connections","Grounding connections only","Neither, if listed fittings are used"],"a":1,"e":"2020 expanded 250.12 to say bonding too. The 2017 text focused on grounding. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 250.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","step":"2017->2020","f":"Sealing raceways exposed to different temperatures: what kind of sealant?","b":"2017 NEC: sealing was with an approved material. 2020 NEC: the sealant must be identified for use with the cable or conductor insulation, bare conductor, shield or other components.","r":"NEC 300.7(A) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 300.7(A), a raceway sealant used where there is a temperature difference must be:","o":["Any approved caulk","Of silicone only","Rated for explosionproof seal use","Identified for use with the conductors and components"],"a":3,"e":"2020 replaced approved with identified, so the sealant must be suitable for the conductor insulation. Explosionproof seals are not required for this purpose. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 300.7(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","step":"2017->2020","f":"Exit enclosures (stair towers): wiring allowed... new section?","b":"2017 NEC: no section limiting wiring in exit enclosures. 2020 NEC: new 300.25 says only wiring serving equipment permitted by the authority having jurisdiction may be installed in exit enclosures, and wiring serving other areas must be outside.","r":"NEC 300.25 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 300.25, what wiring is permitted in an exit enclosure such as a stair tower?","o":["Only Type NM cable","No electrical wiring of any kind","Any building branch circuit wiring","Only wiring serving equipment permitted by the authority having jurisdiction in the enclosure"],"a":3,"e":"300.25 is new for 2020 and limits exit enclosure wiring to equipment permitted there; wiring for other areas goes outside. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 300.25; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","step":"2017->2020","f":"High voltage warning signs: wording change?","b":"2017 NEC: access points over 1000 volts were required to have warning signs reading WARNING. 2020 NEC: the sign wording changed to DANGER.","r":"NEC 300.45 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 300.45, signs at access to conductors over 1000 volts must read:","o":["NOTICE - ENERGIZED","DANGER - HIGH VOLTAGE - KEEP OUT","WARNING - HIGH VOLTAGE - KEEP OUT","CAUTION - HIGH VOLTAGE"],"a":1,"e":"The signal word for over-1000 V signs changed from Warning to Danger in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 300.45; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","step":"2017->2020","f":"Ampacity table numbering: 310.15(B)(16) vs 310.16?","b":"2017 NEC: the 60-90 degree C ampacity table was Table 310.15(B)(16) (with (B)(17)-(B)(21) as the other tables). 2020 NEC: the tables are restored to Table 310.16 through Table 310.21, and the word allowable was dropped from ampacity.","r":"NEC Table 310.16 (2020); Table 310.15(B)(16) (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, the table of ampacities of insulated conductors rated up to 2000 volts, 60 through 90 degrees C (not more than three current-carrying conductors) is numbered:","o":["Table 310.15(B)(16)","Table 310.16","Table 310.12","Table 310.15(C)(1)"],"a":1,"e":"2017 used Table 310.15(B)(16). 2020 renumbered it Table 310.16. Many exam questions reference the table number. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Table 310.16; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","step":"2017->2020","f":"Ambient temperature correction and adjustment factor tables renumbered?","b":"2017 NEC: Table 310.15(B)(2)(a) was ambient temperature correction and Table 310.15(B)(3)(a) was the adjustment factors for more than three current-carrying conductors. 2020 NEC: they are Table 310.15(B)(1) and Table 310.15(C)(1).","r":"NEC Table 310.15(B)(1), Table 310.15(C)(1) (2020); Tables 310.15(B)(2)(a), 310.15(B)(3)(a) (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, the adjustment factors for more than three current-carrying conductors in a raceway appear in:","o":["Table 310.15(C)(1)","Table 310.15(B)(3)(a)","Table 310.16","Table 310.15(B)(1)"],"a":0,"e":"2017 placed adjustment factors in Table 310.15(B)(3)(a). In 2020 they moved to 310.15(C)(1), and the ambient correction table is 310.15(B)(1). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 310.15(B)(1) / 310.15(C)(1); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","step":"2017->2020","f":"83 percent dwelling service rule: where in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: single-phase 120/240 V dwelling service and feeder conductor sizing (83 percent) was in 310.15(B)(7). 2020 NEC: moved to new 310.12, with a new Table 310.12 for 100 to 400 A services, usable when no correction or adjustment factors apply.","r":"NEC 310.12, Table 310.12 (2020); 310.15(B)(7) (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, which section contains the reduced conductor sizing for single-phase dwelling services and feeders?","o":["310.15(B)(7)","310.14","310.16","310.12"],"a":3,"e":"310.15(B)(7) in 2017 became 310.12 in 2020, with a table. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 310.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 311 Medium Voltage Conductors and Cable","step":"2017->2020","f":"Medium-voltage cable: new article, deleted article?","b":"2017 NEC: Type MV cable rules were in Article 328 and over-2000 V rules also sat in Article 310. 2020 NEC: Article 328 is deleted and the contents moved to new Article 311; Article 310 is limited to conductors rated up to 2000 volts.","r":"NEC Article 311, 310.1 (2020); Article 328 (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, medium voltage cable (Type MV) requirements are located in which article?","o":["Article 310 Part IV","Article 311","Article 328","Article 330"],"a":1,"e":"Article 328 was deleted. MV requirements are now in Article 311 and 310 is for 2000 V or less. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Art. 311; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 337 Type P Cable","step":"2017->2020","f":"Marine shipboard cable... Type P new article?","b":"2017 NEC: no article for Type P cable. 2020 NEC: new Article 337 covers Type P cable (600 V, armored or unarmored) limited to industrial installations and hazardous (classified) locations.","r":"NEC Article 337 (2020)","q":"Which new cable article was added to Chapter 3 in the 2020 NEC?","o":["Article 346, RMC","Article 400, Flexible Cords","Article 337, Type P Cable","Article 321, Messenger-Supported Wiring"],"a":2,"e":"Type P (marine shipboard) cable was added in 2020 as Article 337. The others are older articles. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Art. 337; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 312 Cabinets","step":"2017->2020","f":"Power monitoring equipment in switch and OCPD enclosures?","b":"2017 NEC: no express permission for power monitoring or energy management equipment in switch and overcurrent device enclosures. 2020 NEC: 312.8(B) permits power monitoring and energy management equipment in these enclosures.","r":"NEC 312.8(B) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 312.8(B), which equipment is permitted in switch and overcurrent device enclosures?","o":["Auxiliary gutters feeding other loads","Splices and taps of any feeder","Any cable-connected appliance","Power monitoring and control (energy management) equipment"],"a":3,"e":"312.8(B) now explicitly permits power monitoring and energy management equipment. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 312.8(B); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 314 Outlet, Device, Pull, and Junction Boxes","step":"2017->2020","f":"Boxes at paddle fan locations in dwellings... what changed?","b":"2017 NEC: a fan-listed box was required where a spare separately switched ungrounded conductor existed for a future fan. 2020 NEC: in habitable rooms of dwelling units, ceiling outlet boxes where a fan can be installed must be listed for sole support of a ceiling-suspended fan, or comply with 314.27 and have accessible structural framing that can support a fan.","r":"NEC 314.27(C) (2020); 314.27(C) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a ceiling outlet box in a dwelling bedroom must be:","o":["A box rated for 100 lb of load","A pancake box only","Listed for fan support, or a box with accessible structural framing capable of supporting a ceiling fan","A standard box of any type"],"a":2,"e":"2020 expanded fan support to all habitable room ceiling outlets in dwelling units. The 2017 rule was triggered by a spare switched conductor. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 314.27(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 392 Cable Trays","step":"2017->2020","f":"Cable ties in cable tray: must they be listed?","b":"2017 NEC: no requirement that cable ties in cable trays be listed. 2020 NEC: 392.30(B) requires cable ties used to secure cables in a cable tray to be listed and identified for the purpose.","r":"NEC 392.30(B) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 392.30(B), cable ties used to secure cables in cable tray must be:","o":["Listed and identified for the purpose","Any nylon tie","Orange in color","Metallic only"],"a":0,"e":"2020 added a listing requirement for cable ties in tray. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 392.30(B); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 404 Switches","step":"2017->2020","f":"Faceplates for snap switches... now dimmers too?","b":"2017 NEC: 404.9 faceplate and grounding rules were written for snap switches. 2020 NEC: scope covers snap switches, dimmers and control switches.","r":"NEC 404.9 (2020)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, 404.9 provisions on faceplates and grounding apply to snap switches and which other devices?","o":["Dimmers and control switches","Only lampholders","Only circuit breakers","Only receptacles"],"a":0,"e":"404.9 was expanded to dimmers and control switches. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 404.9; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 406 Receptacles","step":"2017->2020","f":"Replacing controlled receptacles?","b":"2017 NEC: 406.4(D) had six items for replacements. 2020 NEC: new 406.4(D)(7) requires an automatically controlled receptacle to be replaced with an equivalent controlled receptacle; if the control is no longer needed, replace the receptacle and faceplate with unmarked versions.","r":"NEC 406.4(D)(7) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 406.4(D)(7), an automatically controlled receptacle must be replaced with:","o":["An equivalently controlled receptacle","A standard receptacle","A GFCI receptacle with no marking","A tamper-resistant receptacle only"],"a":0,"e":"406.4(D)(7) was added in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.4(D)(7); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 406 Receptacles","step":"2017->2020","f":"Receptacles below a sink... face-up?","b":"2017 NEC: face-up countertop restriction only. 2020 NEC: new 406.5(G)(2) prohibits face-up receptacles in the area below a sink.","r":"NEC 406.5(G)(2) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 406.5(G)(2), receptacles in the area below a sink:","o":["Shall not be installed in a face-up position","May be installed face-up with a cover","Must be GFCI only","Are prohibited entirely"],"a":0,"e":"406.5(G)(2) is new in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.5(G)(2); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 406 Receptacles","step":"2017->2020","f":"Receptacles near tubs and showers: the 3 ft by 8 ft zone?","b":"2017 NEC: receptacles were not allowed within or directly over a bathtub or shower stall. 2020 NEC: no receptacles within 3 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically of the tub rim or shower threshold (including the space directly over); exception for bathrooms too small, allowing the farthest wall.","r":"NEC 406.9(C) (2020); 406.9(C) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a receptacle may NOT be installed within what zone of a bathtub rim?","o":["1 ft horizontally and 4 ft vertically","Only within or directly over the tub","6 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically","3 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically"],"a":3,"e":"2017 only barred receptacles within or directly over the tub. 2020 added a 3 ft x 8 ft zone with an exception for small bathrooms. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.9(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 406 Receptacles","step":"2017->2020","f":"Tamper-resistant receptacles... garages and accessory buildings?","b":"2017 NEC: TR receptacles required in dwelling units' 210.52 areas. 2020 NEC: item (1) expressly includes attached and detached garages and accessory buildings of dwelling units and common areas of multifamily dwellings.","r":"NEC 406.12(1) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, tamper-resistant receptacles are required in which location?","o":["Only living rooms","A detached garage of a one-family dwelling","Only bedrooms","Not in garages"],"a":1,"e":"2020 406.12(1) names garages and accessory buildings. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.12(1); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 406 Receptacles","step":"2017->2020","f":"TR receptacles in hotels/motels, schools, assisted living?","b":"2017 NEC: 406.12 items (1) through (7). 2020 NEC: items (1) through (8): hotel and motel common areas added to item (2), educational facilities (the word elementary deleted) in (4), dormitory units in (7), and new item (8) assisted living facilities.","r":"NEC 406.12 (2020)","q":"Which facility was added as new item (8) of 406.12 in the 2020 NEC?","o":["Car dealerships","Assisted living facilities","Parking garages","Warehouses"],"a":1,"e":"406.12(8) is new. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 406 Receptacles","step":"2017->2020","f":"Reconditioned receptacles and plugs?","b":"2017 NEC: no explicit prohibition. 2020 NEC: reconditioned receptacles, attachment plugs, cord connectors and flanged surface devices are prohibited.","r":"NEC 406.3, 406.7 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, reconditioned attachment plugs are:","o":["Allowed with a label","Allowed if tested","Allowed in industrial only","Prohibited"],"a":3,"e":"2020 added the prohibition. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.3 / 406.7; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions","step":"2017->2020","f":"New Article 100 term for field labeling of equipment?","b":"2017 NEC: no Article 100 definition of Field Evaluation Body. 2020 NEC: Field Evaluation Body (FEB) is defined in Article 100 and is used in sections such as 445.6 and 230.66 for field evaluation and labeling of equipment.","r":"NEC Art. 100 Field Evaluation Body (2020); 445.6 (2020)","q":"Which new organization type is defined in Article 100 of the 2020 NEC and referenced for field evaluation of custom or one-of-a-kind equipment such as generators?","o":["Authority Having Jurisdiction Evaluation Board","Qualified Person Committee","Field Evaluation Body (FEB)","Nationally Recognized Testing Laboratory Council"],"a":2,"e":"The FEB definition was added in 2020 so equipment like one-of-a-kind generators (445.6) and field-assembled service equipment (230.66) can be field evaluated and labeled.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 215 Feeders","step":"2017->2020","f":"Minimum feeder rating and size: voltage reference changed?","b":"2017 NEC: 215.2 text referred to feeders rated 600 volts. 2020 NEC: the voltage limit is raised to 1000 volts, and a reference to 110.14(C) was added.","r":"NEC 215.2 (2020); 215.2 (2017)","q":"The 2020 NEC revised the voltage reference in 215.2 from 600 volts to:","o":["1000 volts","2000 volts","480 volts","250 volts"],"a":0,"e":"2020 aligns feeder rules with the 1000 V upper limit used for low-voltage systems. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 215.2; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear, and Panelboards","step":"2017->2020","f":"Reconditioning panelboards and switchgear?","b":"2017 NEC: no section on reconditioning. 2020 NEC: new 408.8 states panelboards may not be reconditioned (replacement of components within the enclosure is permitted), while switchboards and switchgear may be reconditioned if listed or field labeled.","r":"NEC 408.8 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 408.8, which equipment may NOT be reconditioned?","o":["Low-voltage power circuit breakers","Panelboards","Switchboards","Switchgear"],"a":1,"e":"408.8 is new in 2020. Panelboards cannot be reconditioned; switchboards and switchgear can under conditions. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 408.8; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear, and Panelboards","step":"2017->2020","f":"Panelboard orientation?","b":"2017 NEC: no express panelboard orientation restriction. 2020 NEC: new 408.43 prohibits installing panelboards in the face-up position.","r":"NEC 408.43 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 408.43, panelboards:","o":["Must be face-down","May be installed face-up in damp locations","Must be mounted with a vertical orientation only","Shall not be installed in the face-up position"],"a":3,"e":"408.43 is new in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 408.43; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders, and Lamps","step":"2017->2020","f":"Closet storage space definition: clothes closets?","b":"2017 NEC: defined Closet Storage Space. 2020 NEC: term is Clothes Closet Storage Space and 410.16 applies to clothes closets.","r":"NEC 410.2, 410.16 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 410.16, the luminaire rules for closets apply to what?","o":["Clothes closets","Pantries","All storage closets","Linen closets only"],"a":0,"e":"2020 added the word clothes to the definition and rule. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 410.2 / 410.16; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 410 Luminaires, Lampholders, and Lamps","step":"2017->2020","f":"Horticultural lighting: new part in Article 410?","b":"2017 NEC: no horticultural lighting part. 2020 NEC: new Part XVI, Special Provisions for Horticultural Lighting Equipment.","r":"NEC Article 410 Part XVI (2020)","q":"Which new part was added to Article 410 in the 2020 NEC?","o":["Part XVI, Solar Luminaires","Part XVI, Electric Vehicle Charging","Part XVI, Emergency Lighting","Part XVI, Horticultural Lighting Equipment"],"a":3,"e":"Part XVI is new in 2020 for grow lights. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Part XVI (410.170-); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 422 Appliances","step":"2017->2020","f":"GFCI for appliances: voltage and type changed?","b":"2017 NEC: 422.5(A) covered five appliance types at 250 V or less and 60 A or less (with GFCI of unspecified class). 2020 NEC: 150 V or less to ground and 60 A or less, Class A GFCI protection for personnel, seven list items, and an informational note pointing to 210.8.","r":"NEC 422.5(A) (2020); 422.5(A) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 422.5(A), appliances require what type of GFCI protection?","o":["Class A GFCI protection for personnel","Any GFCI","Class B","AFCI"],"a":0,"e":"2020 specified Class A and 150 V to ground. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 422.5(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 430 Motors, Motor Circuits, and Controllers","step":"2017->2020","f":"Motors with built-in electronic overload protection: what term was defined in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: no definition for motors protected by an integral electronic control; only thermally protected motors were defined. 2020 NEC: new definition of Electronically Protected (as applied to motors) in 430.2, and 430.32(A)(2) and (B)(2) now recognize electronically protected motors alongside thermally protected motors.","r":"NEC 430.2, 430.32(A)(2), (B)(2) (2020)","q":"Which motor protection term was newly defined in 430.2 of the 2020 NEC?","o":["Fuse Protected (as applied to motors)","Thermally Mounted (as applied to motors)","Electronically Protected (as applied to motors)","Magnetically Protected (as applied to motors)"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 NEC added the Electronically Protected definition in 430.2 (a motor with an integral electronic control that protects against overheating, failure to start, and similar) and extended 430.32 to cover it. Thermally Protected was already defined; the other choices are not NEC terms.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 445 Generators","step":"2017->2020","f":"Must stationary generators be listed? New 2020 rule?","b":"2017 NEC: Article 445 had no listing requirement for generators. 2020 NEC: new 445.6 requires stationary generators operating at 600 volts or less to be listed; a one-of-a-kind or custom unit may be field labeled by a Field Evaluation Body.","r":"NEC 445.6 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 445.6, stationary generators rated 600 volts or less must be:","o":["Limited to 15 kW or less","Listed (or field labeled by a Field Evaluation Body if custom)","Installed outdoors only","Approved by the owner only"],"a":1,"e":"445.6 is new in 2020 and is why the Field Evaluation Body definition was added. Nothing in 445.6 limits kW size or requires outdoor installation. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 445.6; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 445 Generators","step":"2017->2020","f":"Emergency shutdown for a standby generator at a house... what did 2020 add?","b":"2017 NEC: no emergency shutdown requirement for generators at dwellings in 445.18. 2020 NEC: new 445.18(D) requires a generator installed at a one- or two-family dwelling (other than a cord-and-plug connected portable unit) to have an emergency shutdown device outdoors at a readily accessible location, marked per 110.21(B).","r":"NEC 445.18(D) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a permanently installed standby generator at a one-family dwelling must have an emergency shutdown device located:","o":["At the generator only, inside its enclosure","In the electrical panel only","Inside the garage next to the transfer switch","Outside the dwelling at a readily accessible location"],"a":3,"e":"445.18(D) is new for 2020 and parallels the outdoor emergency disconnect of 230.85. It does not apply to cord-and-plug portable generators. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 445.18(D); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 480 Storage Batteries","step":"2017->2020","f":"Where did the battery interconnection rules from Article 706 go?","b":"2017 NEC: battery interconnection rules were in 706.32 (with other battery rules in Article 706). 2020 NEC: moved to Article 480 (480.12; also 480.4(D), 480.7(C), 480.13), so Article 480 holds battery rules and Article 706 holds system rules.","r":"NEC 480.12 (2020); 706.32 (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, rules for interconnecting battery cells, units, or modules were relocated from Article 706 to:","o":["Article 480 Part III","Article 690","Article 480 (480.12)","Article 710"],"a":2,"e":"Relocation only. The 2017 NEC kept battery interconnection in 706.32. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 480.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","step":"2017->2020","f":"Section 500.4 in 2020: what is the new title?","b":"2017 NEC: 500.4 was titled General. 2020 NEC: retitled Documentation (area classification documentation requirement), with a new informational note.","r":"NEC 500.4 (2020); 500.4 (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, section 500.4 is titled:","o":["General","Classification of Locations","Equipment","Documentation"],"a":3,"e":"2017 titled it General; 2020 changed it to Documentation. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 500.4; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 511 Commercial Garages, Repair and Storage","step":"2017->2020","f":"GFCI in commercial garages: what does 511.12 now reference?","b":"2017 NEC: 511.12 had its own GFCI wording for 125 V, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles in areas with electrical diagnostic equipment. 2020 NEC: 511.12 is expanded by reference to the non-dwelling GFCI list in 210.8(B).","r":"NEC 511.12 (2020); 210.8(B)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, GFCI protection in commercial garages (511.12) is expanded by reference to which section?","o":["680.22","422.5","210.8(A)","210.8(B)"],"a":3,"e":"2020 pointed 511.12 to 210.8(B), which now covers garages, service bays and similar areas. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 511.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 518 Assembly Occupancies","step":"2017->2020","f":"Lighting at outdoor service equipment of theaters and arenas?","b":"2017 NEC: no illumination requirement in Article 518. 2020 NEC: new 518.6 requires illumination of outdoor working spaces about service equipment serving assembly occupancies.","r":"NEC 518.6 (2020)","q":"A new section 518.6 in the 2020 NEC requires illumination of:","o":["Outdoor working space about service equipment serving an assembly occupancy","Exit signs inside the assembly space","Parking lot poles only","Stage rigging"],"a":0,"e":"518.6 is new in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 518.6; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards, Floating Buildings","step":"2017->2020","f":"Marina ground-fault protection: renumbered and split?","b":"2017 NEC: ground-fault protection for marinas was at 555.3. 2020 NEC: relocated to 555.35 (555.3 now covers datum plane distances), split into (A) ground-fault protection and (B) leakage current measurement device, with informational notes.","r":"NEC 555.35 (2020); 555.3 (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, ground-fault protection of equipment for marina feeders and branch circuits is located in:","o":["555.19","555.9","555.3","555.35"],"a":3,"e":"Number moved: 555.3 became 555.35, and the new 555.3 deals with electrical datum plane distances. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 555.35; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 590 Temporary Installations","step":"2017->2020","f":"Reusing overcurrent devices on temporary power: new 2020 section?","b":"2017 NEC: no section on reuse of overcurrent devices for temporary installations. 2020 NEC: new 590.8: reused overcurrent protective devices must be examined for proper installation, maintenance and impending failure; service overcurrent devices in 277/480-volt temporary installations must be current limiting.","r":"NEC 590.8 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 590.8, an overcurrent protective device reused for temporary power must be:","o":["Replaced with a new device every time","Rated for 600 volts","Examined for proper installation, maintenance, and impending failure","Used only for lighting"],"a":2,"e":"590.8 is new for 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 590.8; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","step":"2017->2020","f":"EV article scope and new EVPE definition in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: Article 625 covered EV charging equipment. 2020 NEC: the scope includes systems that permit bidirectional current flow, and 625.2 adds the definition of Electric Vehicle Power Export Equipment (EVPE).","r":"NEC 625.1, 625.2 (2020)","q":"Which new term was defined in 625.2 of the 2020 NEC?","o":["Vehicle Charge Controller","Electric Vehicle Charging Station Operator","Mobile Energy Storage Unit","Electric Vehicle Power Export Equipment (EVPE)"],"a":3,"e":"2020 added EVPE and bidirectional current flow to Article 625, consistent with 90.2(A)(6). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 625.1 / 625.2; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","step":"2017->2020","f":"AC receptacles on the vehicle used to power equipment: new section?","b":"2017 NEC: no section on AC receptacle outlets in electric vehicles. 2020 NEC: new 625.60 addresses AC receptacle outlets in EVs that supply off-board equipment as part of EVPE.","r":"NEC 625.60 (2020)","q":"A new section 625.60 in the 2020 NEC addresses what?","o":["AC receptacle outlets in electric vehicles used for connecting off-board equipment","DC fast charging station signage","Wireless power transfer pads","Overhead charging gantries"],"a":0,"e":"625.60 is new in 2020 and relates to vehicle power export. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 625.60; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","step":"2017->2020","f":"Can the AHJ require pool inspections after installation? 2020 change?","b":"2017 NEC: no AHJ authority stated for periodic inspection of pool and spa installations after the initial inspection. 2020 NEC: 680.4: the authority having jurisdiction may require periodic inspection and testing of pool and spa electrical installations after installation.","r":"NEC 680.4 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 680.4, the authority having jurisdiction may:","o":["Require periodic inspection and testing after installation","Inspect only at the time of permit","Waive GFCI requirements after one year","Not require any follow-up inspections"],"a":0,"e":"680.4 was added in 2020 as Inspections After Installation. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 680.4; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","step":"2017->2020","f":"GFCI for pool pump motors: which circuits in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: GFCI for outlets supplying pool pump motors on single-phase 120- through 240-volt branch circuits rated 15 or 20 amperes. 2020 NEC: GFCI for pool motor circuits rated 150 volts or less to ground and 60 amperes or less, single- or three-phase, with an exception for listed low-voltage pool motors.","r":"NEC 680.21(C) (2020); 680.21(C) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 680.21(C), a 240-volt, single-phase, 50-ampere pool pump motor circuit:","o":["Requires GFCI protection","Is exempt because it exceeds 20 amperes","Is exempt if hard-wired","Requires AFCI protection instead"],"a":0,"e":"2020 extended pool motor GFCI to 150 V-to-ground circuits up to 60 A, single- or three-phase. The 2017 limit was 15 or 20 A. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 680.21(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","step":"2017->2020","f":"Replacing a pool pump motor: new GFCI requirement?","b":"2017 NEC: no requirement to add GFCI protection when replacing a pump motor. 2020 NEC: new 680.21(D) requires GFCI protection per 680.21(C) when a pool pump motor is repaired or replaced.","r":"NEC 680.21(D) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, when an existing pool pump motor is replaced, the circuit:","o":["Needs AFCI protection","Must be upgraded to 60 amperes","Need not be changed","Must be provided with GFCI protection per 680.21(C)"],"a":3,"e":"680.21(D) is new in 2020 and brings existing pool pumps up to GFCI protection when the motor is replaced. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 680.21(D); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Rapid shutdown inside the PV array boundary: listing change?","b":"2017 NEC: 690.12(B)(2)(1) required a PV array that is listed or field labeled as a rapid shutdown PV array. 2020 NEC: requires a listed PV hazard control system (the evaluation standard is UL 3741). The 80 volts within 30 seconds limit inside the array boundary still applies to other methods.","r":"NEC 690.12(B)(2)(1) (2020); 690.12(B)(2)(1) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 690.12(B)(2)(1), the listing-based compliance path inside the array boundary is a:","o":["Field-labeled rapid shutdown PV array","Listed PV hazard control system","Manual disconnect switch","Fusible disconnect at the inverter"],"a":1,"e":"2020 replaced the listed or field labeled rapid shutdown PV array with a listed PV hazard control system. The 30 second time limit existed in 2017 too (the 10 second limit was the 2014 NEC).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"PV equipment grounding conductor sizing: voltage drop upsizing?","b":"2017 NEC: PV EGCs had to be increased in size when ungrounded conductors were increased, including for voltage drop. 2020 NEC: EGCs are no longer required to be increased in size for voltage drop considerations.","r":"NEC 690.45 (2020); 690.45 (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 690.45, must a PV circuit EGC be increased in size because the ungrounded conductors were upsized for voltage drop?","o":["Yes, in the same proportion","Yes, by one AWG size","Yes, only for circuits over 600 volts","No, voltage drop upsizing does not require a larger EGC"],"a":3,"e":"2020 removed the requirement that the EGC be increased for voltage drop. Table 250.122 sizing with the overcurrent device applies. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 690.45; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Meter-mounted transfer switches: where allowed in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: no specific prohibition. 2020 NEC: meter-mounted transfer switches are prohibited for emergency systems (700.5) and legally required standby systems (701.5) but permitted for optional standby systems (702.5(B)) when listed.","r":"NEC 700.5, 701.5, 702.5(B) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, a meter-mounted transfer switch is permitted for which system?","o":["An emergency system","A fire pump power supply","A legally required standby system","An optional standby system"],"a":3,"e":"700.5 and 701.5 prohibit meter-mounted transfer switches; 702.5(B) allows them for optional standby systems. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 700.5; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 701 Legally Required Standby Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Transfer equipment for legally required standby: what changed?","b":"2017 NEC: 701.5 required transfer equipment but did not address meter-mounted devices or reconditioned equipment. 2020 NEC: transfer equipment must be listed, meter-mounted transfer switches are prohibited, and reconditioned transfer equipment is prohibited.","r":"NEC 701.5 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 701.5, transfer equipment for legally required standby systems must be:","o":["Meter-mounted for easy access","Reconditioned if tested","Manually operated only","Listed, and not meter-mounted or reconditioned"],"a":3,"e":"701.5 in 2020 matches 700.5 on meter-mounted switches and adds a ban on reconditioned equipment. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 701.5; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Optional standby transfer equipment: documentation marking?","b":"2017 NEC: no requirement to mark the short-circuit current rating of transfer equipment in 702.5. 2020 NEC: 702.5(C): other than in dwelling units, the short-circuit current rating of transfer equipment must be field marked on the equipment.","r":"NEC 702.5(C) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 702.5(C), outside dwelling units, transfer equipment for optional standby systems must be field marked with its:","o":["Short-circuit current rating","Date of manufacture","Fuel type of the generator","Transfer time in seconds"],"a":0,"e":"702.5 was split into four subsections in 2020: (A) General, (B) meter-mounted transfer switches, (C) Documentation, (D) inadvertent interconnection. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 702.5(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Breaker interlock kits for generator backfeed: listing?","b":"2017 NEC: 702.5 required transfer equipment to prevent inadvertent interconnection but did not state how interlock kits are treated. 2020 NEC: 702.5(D): transfer equipment must be listed and prevent inadvertent interconnection of normal and alternate sources; circuit breaker interlock kits must be listed as transfer equipment.","r":"NEC 702.5(D) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 702.5(D), a circuit breaker interlock kit used to prevent backfeed from a portable generator must be:","o":["Field fabricated by the installer","Listed as transfer equipment","Labeled with a wiring diagram only","Rated for 100 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"2020 added the listing requirement for interlock kits. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 702.5(D); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Signs for standby power at dwellings: where?","b":"2017 NEC: a sign at the service-entrance equipment indicating the type and location of the standby source. 2020 NEC: at one- and two-family dwellings the sign is coordinated with 230.85 and placed at the exterior emergency disconnect.","r":"NEC 702.7(A) (2020); 230.85","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 702.7(A), at a one-family dwelling the sign for an on-site standby power source is placed at:","o":["No sign is required","Inside the garage","Only the generator","The exterior emergency disconnect required by 230.85"],"a":3,"e":"702.7(A) was revised to coordinate with 230.85. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 702.7(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","step":"2017->2020","f":"Supply-side connections: where in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: supply-side source connection rules were in 705.12(A). 2020 NEC: new 705.11 covers supply-side source connections (per 230.82(6)); 705.12 covers load-side connections only.","r":"NEC 705.11, 705.12 (2020); 705.12(A) (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, connection of an interactive power source to the supply side of the service disconnect is covered by:","o":["705.13","705.12","705.11","705.10"],"a":2,"e":"The 2017 NEC covered both supply-side and load-side connections in 705.12. In 2020 supply side is 705.11 and load side is 705.12. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 705.11; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","step":"2017->2020","f":"Power control systems to limit current on busbars?","b":"2017 NEC: no provision for power control systems in Article 705. 2020 NEC: new 705.13 permits a listed power control system (PCS) that monitors current and limits the output of sources and energy storage so the busbar or conductor rating is not exceeded.","r":"NEC 705.13 (2020)","q":"A new 2020 section 705.13 permits the use of:","o":["An AFCI to detect arcing in sources","A lightning rod system","A surge arrester on the DC side","A power control system to limit current on a busbar or conductor"],"a":3,"e":"705.13 is new in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 705.13; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","step":"2017->2020","f":"Directory for power sources: new title?","b":"2017 NEC: 705.10 was titled Directory. 2020 NEC: retitled Identification of Power Sources, with a plaque or directory at service equipment and the location of other power sources.","r":"NEC 705.10 (2020); 705.10 (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, 705.10 is titled:","o":["Point of Connection","Equipment Approval","Identification of Power Sources","Directory"],"a":2,"e":"2020 retitled the section; 710.10 and 712.10 also use Identification of Power Sources or Directory language. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 705.10; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 710 Stand-Alone Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Identification at a building supplied by a stand-alone system?","b":"2017 NEC: no equivalent identification rule in Article 710. 2020 NEC: new 710.10 requires a permanent plaque or directory at a building supplied by a stand-alone system.","r":"NEC 710.10 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 710.10, a building supplied by a stand-alone system requires:","o":["A neutral-to-ground bond at every panel","A surge protective device","A utility meter","A permanent plaque or directory identifying the power sources"],"a":3,"e":"710.10 is new in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 710.10; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 710 Stand-Alone Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Stand-alone inverter input circuit current: where is it?","b":"2017 NEC: 690.8(A)(4) had the calculation for stand-alone inverter input circuit current. 2020 NEC: relocated to 710.12.","r":"NEC 710.12 (2020); 690.8(A)(4) (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, stand-alone inverter input circuit current is addressed in:","o":["710.12","705.28","690.8(A)(4)","706.30"],"a":0,"e":"Relocation only. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 710.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Article 800 and 805 in 2020 vs 2017?","b":"2017 NEC: Article 800 was Communications Circuits. 2020 NEC: Article 800 is General Requirements for Communications Systems, the communications circuits rules moved to new Article 805, and common requirements from 805, 820, 830 and 840 were consolidated in Article 800.","r":"NEC Art. 800, 805 (2020); Art. 800 (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, which article covers communications circuits specifically (telephone and similar)?","o":["Article 805","Article 800","Article 840","Article 820"],"a":0,"e":"In 2017 Article 800 was Communications Circuits. In 2020 Article 800 is the general article, and 805 holds the specific communications circuits rules. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Art. 800 / 805; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Applications of listed communications cables: where in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: permitted cable applications were repeated in 805.154, 820.154, 830.154 and 840.154. 2020 NEC: combined into 800.154; the other articles keep only substitutions.","r":"NEC 800.154 (2020)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, the general rule on applications of listed communications wires, cables and raceways is located in:","o":["840.154","800.154","820.154","805.154"],"a":1,"e":"2020 consolidated permitted applications in 800.154. Article 805, 820 etc. keep the substitution tables. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 800.154; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 820 Community Antenna Television and Radio Distribution Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Overhead coaxial cables: where did the general rule go?","b":"2017 NEC: Article 820 contained the complete overhead (aerial) coaxial cable rules in 820.44. 2020 NEC: except for (A) and (B), the overhead cable requirements are relocated to 800.44.","r":"NEC 800.44, 820.44 (2020); 820.44 (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, the general requirements for overhead (aerial) communications and CATV cables are located in:","o":["800.44","820.44 in full","830.44","840.44"],"a":0,"e":"The general Chapter 8 requirements were consolidated in Article 800 in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 820.44; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2017->2020","f":"Single-pole breakers with handle ties as the service disconnect... still allowed in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: 230.71 allowed up to six service disconnects, and single-pole devices tied together with handle ties could serve as the service disconnect. 2020 NEC: handle-tied single-pole devices are no longer permitted as service disconnects; each service has one disconnecting means unless 230.71(B) applies.","r":"NEC 230.71(A) (2020); 230.71 (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, which arrangement is NOT permitted as a service disconnecting means?","o":["A circuit breaker in a meter-main combination","A two-pole molded-case circuit breaker","Two single-pole circuit breakers with a handle tie serving as the 240-volt service disconnect","A fusible safety switch rated for the service"],"a":2,"e":"Holt notes the 2020 NEC deleted the handle-tie single-pole provisions in 230.71. A listed two-pole breaker or fusible switch is a normal service disconnect. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.71(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2017->2020","f":"Emergency disconnect short-circuit rating requirement in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: no emergency disconnect requirement. 2020 NEC: the outdoor emergency disconnect for one- and two-family dwellings must have a short-circuit current rating equal to or greater than the available fault current, be readily accessible outdoors, and be grouped and marked.","r":"NEC 230.85 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 230.85, the dwelling emergency disconnect must have a short-circuit current rating:","o":["At least equal to the available fault current","No short-circuit rating requirement applies","Of 22,000 amperes only","Of 10,000 amperes regardless of the fault current"],"a":0,"e":"The emergency disconnect is service equipment on the line side of other devices in many installations, so it must be rated for the available fault current (110.9, 230.65).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"AFCI extension exception: how is the 6 ft measured?","b":"2017 NEC: the exception allowed extending a branch circuit up to 6 ft without AFCI if no outlets or devices were added, but it did not clearly exclude conductors inside enclosures or address splicing devices. 2020 NEC: conductors inside an enclosure, cabinet or junction box are not counted toward the 6 ft limit, and a splicing device counts as a device (adding one does not qualify).","r":"NEC 210.12(D) Exception (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 210.12(D) exception, the 6 ft extension limit is measured how?","o":["Including all conductors inside boxes","As the total length of cable removed","There is no length limit in 2020","Excluding conductors inside enclosures, cabinets or junction boxes"],"a":3,"e":"2020 clarified the measurement and treated splicing devices as devices. Check the exception to 210.12(D). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.12(D) Exception; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 406 Receptacles","step":"2017->2020","f":"Tamper-resistant receptacles in schools: any change to elementary?","b":"2017 NEC: 406.12(4) applied to preschools and elementary education facilities. 2020 NEC: the word elementary is deleted, so tamper-resistant receptacles apply in all educational facilities.","r":"NEC 406.12(4) (2020); 406.12(4) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, tamper-resistant receptacles are required in which type of facility under 406.12(4)?","o":["No educational facilities","Elementary schools only","Colleges only","Preschool and all educational facilities, not just elementary schools"],"a":3,"e":"The term elementary was removed in 2020 so the rule covers all educational facilities. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 406.12(4); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 220 Branch-Circuit","step":"2017->2020","f":"Where is the floor area for general lighting loads defined in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: floor area rules were within 220.12. 2020 NEC: new 220.11 holds the floor area rules (outside dimensions of the building, with open porches, garages and unfinished spaces not adaptable for future use excluded for dwellings).","r":"NEC 220.11 (2020); 220.12 (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, which section contains the floor area calculation rule for general lighting loads?","o":["220.14(J)","220.12","220.42","220.11"],"a":3,"e":"The floor area measurement moved from 220.12 to its own section, 220.11, in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 220.11; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","step":"2017->2020","f":"Table 310.12 for dwelling services: when can it be used?","b":"2017 NEC: 310.15(B)(7) allowed reduced sizes for 120/240 V single-phase dwelling services and feeders. 2020 NEC: 310.12 and new Table 310.12 apply only when the conductors supply the entire dwelling load; if more than one service disconnect serves the load, size from Table 310.16 (formerly 310.15(B)(16)).","r":"NEC 310.12, Table 310.12 (2020); 310.15(B)(7) (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC, Table 310.12 for single-phase dwelling services and feeders applies when the conductors supply:","o":["Only 3-phase loads","A single sub-panel","The entire load of the dwelling unit","Only lighting loads"],"a":2,"e":"310.12 reduced sizes (83 percent of rating) are for single-phase 120/240 V conductors carrying the whole dwelling load. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 310.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","step":"2017->2020","f":"New insulation types in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: XHHW family listed. 2020 NEC: XHHN, XHWN and XHWN-2 are added to the permitted uses (dry, damp and wet locations) and to the ampacity tables.","r":"NEC 310.10 (2020)","q":"Which insulation types were added to Article 310 in the 2020 NEC?","o":["MI-2 and MTW-2","RHW-3, RHH-3","THHN-3, THWN-4","XHHN, XHWN and XHWN-2"],"a":3,"e":"ez-pdh notes the 2020 Article 310 additions of XHHN, XHWN and XHWN-2. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 310.10 / Tables; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 311 Medium Voltage Conductors and Cable","step":"2017->2020","f":"Voltage range of the new MV cable ampacity tables?","b":"2017 NEC: MV ampacities were in Article 310. 2020 NEC: new Article 311 includes insulation and ampacity tables for conductors rated 2001 to 35,000 volts.","r":"NEC Article 311 (2020)","q":"The new ampacity tables in the 2020 NEC Article 311 cover conductors rated:","o":["601 to 1000 volts","1001 to 2000 volts","Over 100,000 volts","2001 to 35,000 volts"],"a":3,"e":"Article 311 gathers MV cable rules; Article 310 is limited to 2000 volts and below. Find it in the 2020 NEC at Art. 311 Tables; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards, Floating Buildings","step":"2017->2020","f":"Electrical datum plane distances: new section?","b":"2017 NEC: no section for datum plane distances. 2020 NEC: new 555.3 gives electrical datum plane distances for floating piers, tidal areas and non-tidal areas.","r":"NEC 555.3 (2020)","q":"The new 2020 section 555.3 gives:","o":["Boat hoist wiring methods","Electrical datum plane distances for floating piers and tidal and non-tidal areas","Maximum voltage for shore power","Ground-fault protection requirements"],"a":1,"e":"555.3 is new; the ground-fault protection rule from 2017 555.3 is now 555.35. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 555.3; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","step":"2017->2020","f":"Wiring methods for interactive sources: new section?","b":"2017 NEC: Article 705 had no dedicated section listing permitted wiring methods. 2020 NEC: new 705.25 specifies permitted wiring methods.","r":"NEC 705.25 (2020)","q":"A new 2020 section in Article 705 that specifies permitted wiring methods is:","o":["705.13","705.25","705.20","705.40"],"a":1,"e":"705.25 is new; 705.13 is power control systems. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 705.25; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2017->2020","f":"Field-assembled service equipment: evaluation by FEB?","b":"2017 NEC: service equipment had to be listed. 2020 NEC: 230.66 permits field-assembled service equipment to be evaluated by a Field Evaluation Body.","r":"NEC 230.66 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 230.66, service equipment may be field evaluated by:","o":["The utility","The manufacturer only","A Field Evaluation Body","The owner"],"a":2,"e":"FEB was added in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.66; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","step":"2017->2020","f":"Reconditioned fuseholders?","b":"2017 NEC: no explicit rule. 2020 NEC: 240.62 prohibits reconditioning low-voltage fuseholders and nonrenewable fuses.","r":"NEC 240.62 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 240.62, reconditioning of low-voltage fuseholders and nonrenewable fuses is:","o":["Allowed for 600 V only","Allowed with a label","Allowed","Prohibited"],"a":3,"e":"240.62 was added in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 240.62; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 338 Service-Entrance Cable","step":"2017->2020","f":"Service-entrance conductor assembly: new definition?","b":"2017 NEC: no such definition. 2020 NEC: Service-Entrance Conductor Assembly is defined in 338.2 and the cable definition clarifies an overall covering.","r":"NEC 338.2 (2020)","q":"Which new term was defined in 338.2 of the 2020 NEC?","o":["Service Drop Cable","Entrance Raceway Assembly","Service-Entrance Conductor Assembly","Meter Base Assembly"],"a":2,"e":"Added in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 338.2; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","step":"2017->2020","f":"Applicability of Part II wiring and protection in non-patient care areas?","b":"2017 NEC: 517.10(B) listed areas excluded from Part II; AHJs sometimes applied patient care wiring to non-patient areas. 2020 NEC: new list item (3) excludes additional areas from Part II.","r":"NEC 517.10(B)(3) (2020)","q":"A new 2020 list item 517.10(B)(3) excludes additional areas from:","o":["The 210.8 GFCI rules","Article 700","Chapter 3 wiring methods","The Part II wiring and protection requirements for patient care"],"a":3,"e":"Added in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 517.10(B)(3); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","step":"2017->2020","f":"EVSE definition revised in 2020 to include what?","b":"2017 NEC: EVSE definition did not mention personnel protection. 2020 NEC: Electric Vehicle Supply Equipment is revised to include the personnel protection system.","r":"NEC 625.2 (2020)","q":"The 2020 NEC definition of EVSE was revised to include:","o":["The wireless pad only","The utility meter","The personnel protection system","The vehicle battery"],"a":2,"e":"Revised in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 625.2 EVSE; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2017->2020","f":"Lighting outlet control: 'wall switch' wording changed in 2020?","b":"2017 NEC: 210.70 required lighting outlets to be controlled by a wall switch. 2020 NEC: the phrase wall switch is replaced by listed wall-mounted control device, and 210.70(C) requires a control device at each point of entry where required.","r":"NEC 210.70 (2020); 210.70 (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 210.70, the required control of lighting outlets is by a:","o":["Wall switch only; occupancy sensors are not allowed","Pull chain on the luminaire","Listed wall-mounted control device","Plug-in timer"],"a":2,"e":"2020 recognized listed wall-mounted control devices (such as smart controls) instead of only wall switches. Find it in 210.70(A) through (C). Find it in the 2020 NEC at 210.70; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2017->2020","f":"Equipment permitted on the supply side of the service disconnect: what was added?","b":"2017 NEC: 230.82 listed equipment permitted connected to the supply side of the service disconnect (such as meters, surge arresters, taps for fire pumps). 2020 NEC: adds emergency disconnects (230.85) and meter-mounted transfer switches to that list.","r":"NEC 230.82 (2020); 230.82 (2017)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 230.82, which equipment was added to the list of equipment permitted on the supply side of the service disconnect?","o":["Lighting contactors","Ground-fault circuit interrupter receptacles","Emergency disconnects and meter-mounted transfer switches","Branch-circuit panelboards"],"a":2,"e":"230.82 was updated to correlate with the new 230.85 and 702.5(B). Look at 230.82 in the 2020 book. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 230.82; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 312 Cabinets","step":"2017->2020","f":"Conductor deflection in meter sockets?","b":"2017 NEC: the deflection rules of 312.6 did not expressly cover meter socket enclosures. 2020 NEC: the conductor deflection rule applies to meter socket enclosures.","r":"NEC 312.6 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 312.6, the wire-bending and deflection rules apply to which additional enclosures?","o":["Surface raceways","Cable tray","Meter socket enclosures","Pull boxes over 1000 volts only"],"a":2,"e":"2020 added meter socket enclosures to 312.6. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 312.6; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 320 Armored Cable","step":"2017->2020","f":"Type AC in thermal insulation: ampacity adjustment?","b":"2017 NEC: Type AC cable in thermal insulation was limited to the 60 degree C ampacity but adjustment factors for bundled cables were not expressly required. 2020 NEC: ampacity adjustment per 310.15(C)(1) is required when more than two AC cables are installed contiguous in thermal insulation without spacing.","r":"NEC 320.80(A) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 320.80(A), when more than two Type AC cables are installed in contact with thermal insulation without spacing, ampacity must be:","o":["Ignored because AC cable is armored","Adjusted per 310.15(C)(1)","Increased 20 percent","Taken from Table 310.15(B)(1) only"],"a":1,"e":"The same change was made to Type MC cable in 330.80(C). Look at 320.80(A) in the 2020 book. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 320.80(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 330 Metal-Clad Cable","step":"2017->2020","f":"Type MC in thermal insulation: ampacity adjustment?","b":"2017 NEC: Type MC cable in thermal insulation had no express adjustment factor requirement. 2020 NEC: 330.80(C) requires the 310.15(C)(1) adjustment factors when more than two MC cables are installed contiguous in thermal insulation without spacing.","r":"NEC 330.80(C) (2020)","q":"The 2020 NEC added what ampacity requirement for Type MC cables in thermal insulation in 330.80(C)?","o":["Apply only the temperature correction in 310.15(B)(1)","Apply the adjustment factors of 310.15(C)(1) when more than two cables are installed together without spacing","Reduce ampacity by 50 percent","No adjustment is ever needed"],"a":1,"e":"Parallels the 320.80(A) change for Type AC cable. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 330.80(C); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 450 Transformers and Transformer Vaults","step":"2017->2020","f":"Remote disconnect for transformers: lockable?","b":"2017 NEC: 450.14 did not require remote disconnects to be lockable in the open position. 2020 NEC: a remote disconnecting means must be lockable in the open position.","r":"NEC 450.14 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 450.14, a remote disconnecting means for a transformer must be:","o":["Interlocked with the primary fuse","Marked only","Lockable in the open position","Lockable in the closed position"],"a":2,"e":"2020 aligned several disconnect sections (422.31, 424.19, 450.14, 620.51) with the lockable-open rule of 110.25. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 450.14; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 620 Elevators, Escalators, and Moving Walks","step":"2017->2020","f":"Elevator disconnects: lockable in which position?","b":"2017 NEC: less specific wording on which position the disconnect lock must secure. 2020 NEC: 620.51(A) requires the disconnecting means to be lockable only in the open position.","r":"NEC 620.51(A) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 620.51(A), an elevator main disconnecting means must be lockable:","o":["In either position","It need not be lockable","In the closed position only","In the open position only"],"a":3,"e":"2020 aligned elevator disconnects with the lockable-in-open-position rule. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 620.51(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","step":"2017->2020","f":"Article 680 GFCI and 210.8: additive?","b":"2017 NEC: 680.5 covered GFCI protection where indicated in Article 680. 2020 NEC: GFCI requirements in Article 680 are in addition to those in 210.8.","r":"NEC 680.5 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 680.5, GFCI protection required by Article 680 is:","o":["Limited to 125-volt receptacles","In addition to the GFCI requirements of 210.8","In place of the requirements of 210.8","Optional where 210.8 applies"],"a":1,"e":"Check 680.5 in the 2020 book. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 680.5; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Rapid shutdown and AC output circuits that start in the array?","b":"2017 NEC: 690.12 was less clear on AC inverter output circuits originating in the array. 2020 NEC: it is clarified that AC inverter output circuits originating in the array (such as microinverter outputs) must be shut down by rapid shutdown.","r":"NEC 690.12 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 690.12, AC inverter output circuits that originate in the PV array:","o":["Must be grounded","Must have a 10-second delay","Must be shut down by the rapid shutdown function","Are exempt from rapid shutdown"],"a":2,"e":"2020 clarified that circuits originating in the array are controlled conductors for rapid shutdown. Look at 690.12. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 690.12; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Optional standby definition: stored power?","b":"2017 NEC: the optional standby definition was based on on-site generated power. 2020 NEC: the definition is expanded to include stored power as well as on-site generated power.","r":"NEC 702.2 (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 702.2, an optional standby system supplies power from on-site generated power or:","o":["Only a second utility service","Only the utility","Stored power","Fuel cell only"],"a":2,"e":"2020 broadened the definition. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 702.2; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","step":"2017->2020","f":"Unbalanced interconnections: moved?","b":"2017 NEC: unbalanced interconnection requirements were in 705.100. 2020 NEC: relocated to 705.45 and apply to both single-phase and three-phase interconnections.","r":"NEC 705.45 (2020); 705.100 (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, the requirements for unbalanced interconnections are located in:","o":["705.100","705.40","705.45","705.12"],"a":2,"e":"Article 705 was restructured in 2020 and Parts II and III deleted. Find 705.45. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 705.45; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Plenum, riser and general-purpose cable requirements: where?","b":"2017 NEC: each Chapter 8 article repeated cable listing rules (805.179, 820.179, 830.179). 2020 NEC: redundant requirements moved to 800.179 as general requirements.","r":"NEC 800.179 (2020); 805.179, 820.179, 830.179 (2017)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, general requirements for plenum, riser, general-purpose and limited-use communications cables are in:","o":["820.179 only","805.179 only","725.179","800.179"],"a":3,"e":"Consolidated in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 800.179; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 800 General Requirements for Communications Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Grounding and bonding requirements for communications: consolidated?","b":"2017 NEC: metal entrance conduit grounding (800.49, 820.49, 840.49) and cable bonding and grounding (800.100, 820.100) were repeated. 2020 NEC: moved to 800.49 and 800.100 and apply to all Chapter 8 systems except Article 810.","r":"NEC 800.49, 800.100 (2020)","q":"In the 2020 NEC, the bonding and grounding of communications cables and primary protectors for all Chapter 8 systems (except Article 810) is covered in:","o":["820.100","800.100","810.21","805.100"],"a":1,"e":"Consolidated in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 800.49 / 800.100; the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 702 Optional Standby Systems","step":"2017->2020","f":"Reconditioned transfer switches for standby systems?","b":"2017 NEC: no prohibition. 2020 NEC: 702.5(A): transfer equipment is required for standby systems and reconditioned transfer switches are not permitted.","r":"NEC 702.5(A) (2020)","q":"Under the 2020 NEC 702.5(A), reconditioned transfer switches are:","o":["Permitted for optional standby only","Not permitted","Permitted for generators over 20 kW","Permitted with a label"],"a":1,"e":"Added in 2020. Find it in the 2020 NEC at 702.5(A); the Ref line shows where the 2017 text sat. The usual trap is answering with the older 2017 rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions","step":"2020->2023","f":"New definition in 2023: Special Purpose GFCI (SPGFCI). What is it for?","b":"2020: only the GFCI was defined, and no device was defined for circuits above 150 V to ground. 2023: the SPGFCI is a defined device (based on UL 943C) intended for protection of circuits with more than 150 V to ground, used where Article 680 (and 410.184) now require ground-fault protection on those higher-voltage circuits.","r":"NEC 100, 680.5(C) (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, a special purpose GFCI (SPGFCI) is the protection device used for which circuits?","o":["Circuits with more than 150 volts to ground where ground-fault protection is required, such as certain pool equipment circuits","Only circuits of 50 volts or less","Only AFCI-protected bedroom circuits","Only 120-volt receptacle circuits in dwelling units"],"a":0,"e":"The 2023 NEC added the SPGFCI definition and uses it in 680.5(C) for circuits rated over 150 V to ground (not exceeding 480 V). Class A GFCIs (210.8, 680.5(B)) remain the device for 150 V or less to ground. Look up the term in Article 100 and then read 680.5.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions","step":"2020->2023","f":"New 2023 definitions: weight-supporting ceiling receptacle (WSCR) and weight-supporting attachment fitting (WSAF)","b":"2020: ceiling-suspended fan and luminaire support was written without these defined terms. 2023: Article 100 defines the weight-supporting ceiling receptacle and weight-supporting attachment fitting, and 314.27 and 422.18 use the terms for devices identified to support the weight of ceiling-suspended equipment such as paddle fans.","r":"NEC 100, 314.27(E), 422.18 (2023)","q":"A weight-supporting ceiling receptacle (WSCR) under the 2023 NEC is a device intended to do what?","o":["Act as the disconnecting means for a ceiling fan","Provide GFCI protection for ceiling outlets","Serve as the required lighting outlet in a closet","Support the weight of ceiling-suspended equipment such as a luminaire or paddle fan, within its listed limits"],"a":3,"e":"The WSCR and WSAF terms were added so that boxes, fans and luminaires can be matched to listed support ratings. They are about mechanical support, not ground-fault protection or disconnecting. See Article 100, 314.27(E) and 422.18.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","step":"2020->2023","f":"Cybersecurity added to equipment examination criteria in 110.3(A)","b":"2020: 110.3(A) listed factors for judging equipment (suitability, mechanical strength, wire-bending space, and so on) with no mention of cybersecurity. 2023: a new 110.3(A)(8) adds cybersecurity of network-connected life safety equipment as a consideration.","r":"NEC 110.3(A)(8) (2023)","q":"Which of these was newly added to the 110.3(A) list of equipment examination considerations in the 2023 NEC?","o":["Color of the equipment enclosure","Equipment weight in pounds","Cybersecurity of network-connected life safety equipment","Manufacturer's country of origin"],"a":2,"e":"The 2023 NEC added 110.3(A)(8) so that approval of network-connected life safety equipment considers cybersecurity. It is a new item, not in the 2020 list. Find it in 110.3(A).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","step":"2020->2023","f":"110.3(B): 'identified' added; instructions by QR code or web address","b":"2020: equipment had to be installed and used per its listing or labeling instructions. 2023: 110.3(B) also covers equipment that is 'identified' (not just listed or labeled), and an informational note recognizes that instructions may be delivered by printed material, a QR code, or an internet address.","r":"NEC 110.3(B) (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, an informational note to 110.3(B) recognizes that installation and use instructions for equipment may be provided in which of these ways?","o":["Only as printed material attached to the equipment","Only as a verbal instruction from the inspector","Printed material, a QR code, or an internet address","Only on the equipment nameplate"],"a":2,"e":"The 2023 informational note acknowledges electronic delivery of instructions (QR code or web address). The enforceable rule is still that listed, labeled, or identified equipment be installed and used per its instructions. See 110.3(B).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","step":"2020->2023","f":"Arc-flash label on service and feeder equipment: threshold change in 110.16(B)","b":"2020: in other than dwelling units, only service equipment rated 1200 A or more had to carry the arc-flash warning label. 2023: the requirement is expanded to service equipment and feeder-supplied equipment rated 1000 A or more.","r":"NEC 110.16(B) (2023); 110.16(B) (2020)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, in other than dwelling units, arc-flash warning labels are required on service equipment and feeder-supplied equipment rated at least:","o":["600 amperes","1200 amperes","2000 amperes","1000 amperes"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC applied 110.16(B) only to service equipment of 1200 A or more (the 1200 A figure is the tempting old answer). The 2023 NEC lowered the threshold to 1000 A and added feeder-supplied equipment. Look in 110.16(B).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","step":"2020->2023","f":"New section 110.20: reconditioned equipment rules","b":"2020: reconditioned-equipment provisions were limited (for example the marking rule in 110.21(A)(2)). 2023: new 110.20 permits reconditioning unless the NEC prohibits it for that equipment. Equipment that must be listed must be listed or field-labeled as reconditioned (A); equipment not required to be listed must be listed, field-labeled, or reconditioned per the original manufacturer's instructions (B); the AHJ may approve it where those options are unavailable (C).","r":"NEC 110.20 (2023)","q":"Under 2023 NEC 110.20, equipment that is required to be listed and has been reconditioned must be:","o":["Listed or field-labeled as reconditioned","Exempt from any further listing requirements","Approved verbally by the supplier","Labeled with only the original listing mark"],"a":0,"e":"110.20(A) requires reconditioned equipment that must be listed to be listed or field-labeled as reconditioned. 110.20(B) covers equipment not required to be listed, and (C) allows AHJ approval when (A) or (B) cannot be met. Many articles also prohibit reconditioning certain products outright.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","step":"2020->2023","f":"Which devices may NOT be reconditioned under the 2023 NEC?","b":"2020: general reconditioned-equipment marking rules existed but there was no broad list of prohibited items. 2023: reconditioning is prohibited for personnel and equipment protection devices such as GFCIs, AFCIs, ground-fault protection equipment, and surge-protective devices, as well as items like molded-case circuit breakers and low-voltage fuseholders (see 210.2, 240.2 and the other articles' reconditioning sections). Low-voltage power circuit breakers and electromechanical relays with CTs may be reconditioned.","r":"NEC 210.2, 240.2, 110.20 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, which of these may NOT be reconditioned?","o":["A low-voltage power circuit breaker","An electromechanical protective relay with current transformers","A switchboard section, if the article permits it and it is relabeled","A GFCI device"],"a":3,"e":"2023 article-level reconditioning sections (210.2, 240.2 and others) prohibit reconditioning GFCI, AFCI and GFP equipment, among others. 240.2 specifically permits reconditioning of low-voltage power circuit breakers and electromechanical protective relays with current transformers. Check the .2 reconditioned-equipment section of the article involved.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","step":"2020->2023","f":"Open equipment doors and the working-space egress path","b":"2020: no clear rule about equipment doors reducing the egress path from the working space. 2023: 110.26(A)(4) requires that opening equipment doors not reduce the egress path below 24 in. wide and 6-1/2 ft high; if they would, the opening must be large enough that the doors do not block the path.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(4) (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, equipment doors that open into the working space must not reduce the egress path below what minimum width?","o":["36 inches","30 inches","24 inches (with a 6-1/2 ft height)","12 inches"],"a":2,"e":"The 2023 NEC added the egress consideration in 110.26(A)(4): 24 in. wide by 6-1/2 ft high. The 30-inch figure is the minimum working-space width in 110.26(A)(2) and the 36-inch figure is a common depth, so they are tempting but wrong. Look in 110.26(A)(4).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","step":"2020->2023","f":"Working space floor must be level and flat","b":"2020: working space depth, width and height were specified, but not the condition of the floor or platform. 2023: 110.26(A)(6) (and 110.34(A) for over 1000 V) require the working-space floor, grade, or platform to be kept clear and as level and flat as practicable for the full required depth and width.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(6) (2023)","q":"In the 2023 NEC, the floor or grade within the required working space must be:","o":["As level and flat as practicable for the full depth and width of the space","Covered by a rubber mat listed for electrical use","Sloped slightly away from the equipment for drainage","Concrete at least 4 inches thick"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC gave working space dimensions but did not mention the condition of the floor. The 2023 NEC added that the floor, grade or platform be level and flat as practicable for the full depth and width. It does not require concrete, a slope for drainage or rubber mats. Look in 110.26(A)(6).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2020->2023","f":"210.8 terminology: 'listed Class A GFCI'","b":"2020: 210.8 said 'ground-fault circuit-interrupter protection for personnel.' 2023: the text says 'listed Class A GFCI' throughout 210.8; a Class A device trips at a nominal 5 mA (4 to 6 mA).","r":"NEC 210.8 (2023)","q":"A 'Class A' GFCI, as referenced in the 2023 NEC 210.8, trips when the ground-fault current reaches about:","o":["100 milliamperes","30 milliamperes","5 milliamperes","500 milliamperes"],"a":2,"e":"The 2023 NEC 210.8 uses 'listed Class A GFCI' for personnel protection. Class A devices trip at a nominal 5 mA (range 4 to 6 mA). 30 mA is typical equipment-protection range and 100 to 500 mA is far above personnel protection. Look in 210.8 and the Article 100 definition.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2020->2023","f":"Dwelling unit kitchens: which receptacles need GFCI in 2023?","b":"2020: GFCI protection was required for kitchen receptacles that serve countertop surfaces. 2023: 210.8(A)(6) requires GFCI protection for all 125 V through 250 V receptacles in a dwelling unit kitchen on single-phase branch circuits rated 150 V or less to ground, regardless of what they serve or where they are.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(6) (2023); 210.8(A)(6) (2020)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, which receptacles in a dwelling unit kitchen require GFCI protection?","o":["Only those within 6 feet of the sink","All 125 V through 250 V receptacles in the kitchen (single-phase, 150 V or less to ground)","Only those serving the countertop surfaces","Only the 15-ampere receptacles above the counter"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC limited kitchen GFCI to receptacles serving countertops, which is the tempting old answer. The 2023 NEC removed that limitation, so the refrigerator, microwave and range-hood receptacles are covered too. Look in 210.8(A)(6).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2020->2023","f":"GFCI in dwelling areas with a sink and food or beverage preparation (wet bars)","b":"2020: GFCI was required for receptacles within 6 ft of a sink outside the kitchen. 2023: new 210.8(A)(7) covers areas with a sink and permanent provisions for food preparation, beverage preparation, or cooking (for example a wet bar), without the distance-to-sink test.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(7) (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, which dwelling unit location gets GFCI protection under the new 210.8(A)(7)?","o":["Only the main living room","An area with a sink and permanent provisions for food preparation, beverage preparation, or cooking, such as a wet bar","Only a bedroom with a bathroom attached","Only the kitchen"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC added a separate list item for sink areas with permanent food, beverage or cooking provisions. The kitchen itself is item (A)(6). Look in 210.8(A)(6) and (A)(7).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2020->2023","f":"New non-dwelling GFCI locations: buffet/beverage areas and sink areas with food prep","b":"2020: non-dwelling GFCI locations did not include buffet serving areas or break-room style sink areas unless they were a 'kitchen' with permanent cooking provisions. 2023: 210.8(B) adds areas with a sink and permanent provisions for food prep, beverage prep, or cooking, plus buffet and beverage serving areas.","r":"NEC 210.8(B) (2023)","q":"Which of these locations was added to the non-dwelling GFCI list in 210.8(B) in the 2023 NEC?","o":["A storage closet with no sink","A buffet or beverage serving area with permanent provisions for food or beverage preparation","An interior hallway","A mechanical room with no water source"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC broadened non-dwelling GFCI beyond classic kitchens to cover places such as break rooms, coffee kiosks and buffet areas. Dry spaces with no sink or food provisions are not added. See 210.8(B).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2020->2023","f":"Garage branch circuits: additional circuits and equipment","b":"2020: 210.11(C)(4) required at least one 120 V, 20 A branch circuit for receptacles in each vehicle bay and said that circuit had no other outlets. 2023: language clarifies that additional branch circuits of 15 A or larger may supply receptacles or other loads in the garage, and for a single-vehicle-bay garage the required circuit may also supply other equipment within branch-circuit limits.","r":"NEC 210.11(C)(4) (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 210.11(C)(4) clarification, additional branch circuits in a dwelling garage beyond the required 20-ampere garage receptacle circuit:","o":["Must be GFCI-only 15-ampere circuits","Must be supplied by the laundry circuit","Are prohibited in any garage","Are permitted to serve other receptacles and loads in the garage"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 text required at least one 20 A garage circuit with no other outlets, so some read it as prohibiting any other garage load. The 2023 clarification confirms that additional branch circuits may be installed and that in a one-vehicle-bay garage the required circuit may serve other equipment within limits. Look in 210.11(C)(4).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2020->2023","f":"Kitchen island and peninsula receptacles no longer required","b":"2020: at least one receptacle was required for an island countertop of 12 in. by 24 in. or more and for a peninsula. 2023: island and peninsula receptacles are not required, but if none is installed provisions must be made for a future receptacle (such as a raceway or cable to a covered box).","r":"NEC 210.52(C)(2) (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, if no receptacle is installed at a dwelling kitchen island countertop, what is required?","o":["Provisions for a receptacle to be added in the future","A GFCI receptacle on the island's side face","A dedicated 30-ampere circuit","Nothing at all"],"a":0,"e":"The 2023 NEC removed the mandatory island/peninsula receptacle but requires a future-use provision, not nothing. The 2020 rule required at least one receptacle. Look in 210.52(C)(2).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2020->2023","f":"Receptacle location relative to countertop: no receptacles below the surface","b":"2020: receptacles could be mounted up to 20 in. above or up to 12 in. below the countertop or work surface in certain cases. 2023: receptacles are not permitted below the countertop or work surface; they must be on or above it (no more than 20 in. above) or in a listed countertop or work surface assembly.","r":"NEC 210.52(C)(3) (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, where may a required kitchen countertop receptacle NOT be installed?","o":["On a backsplash wall within reach","Below the countertop or work surface","Within 20 inches above the countertop","In a listed pop-up assembly in the countertop"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC allowed receptacles up to 12 in. below the countertop in certain situations; the 2023 NEC removed that, requiring receptacles to be on or above the surface (not over 20 in. above) or in a listed assembly. Receptacles within 20 inches above and listed pop-up assemblies remain permitted. Look in 210.52(C)(3).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2020->2023","f":"Lighting controls cannot depend only on batteries","b":"2020: no rule against a switch or wall control that depends solely on a battery. 2023: switches and wall-mounted controls required by 210.70(A), (B), and (C) cannot rely exclusively on a battery unless the lighting outlets automatically turn on when the battery fails.","r":"NEC 210.70 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, a wall-mounted control for a required lighting outlet may rely only on batteries if:","o":["It is installed in a garage","The control is not accessible to children","The battery is a lithium type","The lighting outlet automatically energizes upon battery failure"],"a":3,"e":"In 2020 a required lighting control could be a battery-dependent device with no fail-safe. The 2023 NEC says switches or wall controls for the 210.70(A), (B) and (C) lighting outlets cannot rely exclusively on a battery unless the lighting outlet automatically turns on if the battery fails. The battery type, location, or child access are not the test. Look in 210.70.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 215 Feeders","step":"2020->2023","f":"New 215.15: barriers in equipment supplied by feeder taps or transformer secondaries","b":"2020: no feeder-side barrier rule. 2023: 215.15 requires barriers so that no energized, uninsulated busbar or terminal is exposed to inadvertent contact when servicing load terminations with the disconnect open, in equipment supplied by feeder taps (240.21(B)) or transformer secondary conductors (240.21(C)).","r":"NEC 215.15 (2023)","q":"Under new 2023 NEC 215.15, barriers are required in equipment supplied by feeder taps or transformer secondary conductors to prevent:","o":["Overheating of the disconnect handle","Interference from nearby radio equipment","Corrosion of terminals","Inadvertent contact with energized line-side busbars or terminals when servicing the load terminations"],"a":3,"e":"215.15 mirrors the service rule in 230.62(C), applied to feeders. It protects workers from line-side parts that stay energized even with the disconnect open. See 215.15.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 215 Feeders","step":"2020->2023","f":"New 215.18: surge protection on feeders","b":"2020: surge protection was required only at dwelling unit services (230.67). 2023: 215.18 requires a Type 1 or Type 2 SPD with an In rating of at least 10 kA on the load side of the feeder overcurrent device where a feeder supplies dwelling units, dormitory units, guest rooms and suites, or patient sleeping rooms in nursing and limited-care facilities.","r":"NEC 215.18 (2023); 230.67 (2020)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 215.18, an SPD on a feeder supplying dwelling units must have a minimum nominal discharge current (In) rating of:","o":["10 kA","3 kA","5 kA","20 kA"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC required service SPDs for dwelling units only (230.67); feeder SPDs did not exist. The 2023 NEC added 215.18 for feeders and set the same minimum 10 kA nominal discharge current (In), Type 1 or Type 2, installed in or adjacent to the distribution equipment on the load side of the feeder overcurrent device. 3 kA and 5 kA are lower values that do not meet the rule, and 20 kA is not the minimum. Look in 215.18.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 220 Branch-Circuit","step":"2020->2023","f":"New 220.57: EVSE load in load calculations","b":"2020: no specific EVSE load rule in Article 220 (EVSE was a continuous load per Article 625). 2023: 220.57 requires the EVSE load to be calculated as the larger of 7200 W or the EVSE nameplate rating.","r":"NEC 220.57 (2023)","q":"Under 2023 NEC 220.57, the load for an EVSE in a service or feeder calculation is the larger of the nameplate rating or:","o":["5000 watts","3600 watts","7200 watts","9600 watts"],"a":2,"e":"In the 2020 NEC there was no 220.57; EVSE loads were treated as continuous loads under Article 625 and were often added at nameplate. The 2023 NEC added 220.57 so the EVSE load is the larger of 7200 W or the nameplate rating, which prevents under-counting low-rated chargers. 3600 W and 5000 W are lower than the minimum and 9600 W is not the floor. Look in 220.57.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 220 Branch-Circuit","step":"2020->2023","f":"New 220.70: energy management systems and load calculations","b":"2020: Article 750 (EMS) existed but Article 220 had no load-calculation provision for it. 2023: 220.70 allows an energy management system that limits service or feeder current to be used in the calculation, using the EMS maximum setpoint as a continuous load.","r":"NEC 220.70, 750.30 (2023)","q":"Under the new 2023 NEC 220.70, when an energy management system limits the maximum current on a service or feeder, the calculated load is based on:","o":["50 percent of the service rating","The EMS maximum setpoint, treated as a continuous load","The lowest nameplate rating of any load","The sum of all connected loads regardless of the EMS"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC created Article 750 for energy management systems, but Article 220 did not tell you how to count the EMS in a load calculation. The 2023 NEC added 220.70: when an EMS limits the maximum current, the calculation uses the EMS maximum setpoint as a continuous load. Summing all connected loads ignores the EMS, 50 percent of the service is arbitrary, and the lowest nameplate is not the method. Look in 220.70 and 750.30.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","step":"2020->2023","f":"New 225.41: outdoor emergency disconnect for feeders to dwellings","b":"2020: 230.85 required an outdoor emergency disconnect only for services to one- and two-family dwellings. 2023: new 225.41 applies similar requirements to feeders supplying one- and two-family dwellings or other structures (disconnect outdoors, readily accessible, marked 'EMERGENCY DISCONNECT').","r":"NEC 225.41 (2023); 230.85 (2020)","q":"The 2023 NEC added 225.41. What does it require?","o":["An outdoor emergency disconnect for feeders supplying one- and two-family dwellings","An outdoor GFCI receptacle on every feeder","An arc-flash label on every panelboard","A second grounding electrode at each structure"],"a":0,"e":"230.85 (2020) covered services. 225.41 (2023) brings a similar outdoor emergency disconnect requirement to feeders supplying one- and two-family dwellings or other structures. A GFCI receptacle, arc-flash label or second electrode is not what the section requires. Look in 225.41.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","step":"2020->2023","f":"New 225.42: surge protection for outside feeders","b":"2020: no outside feeder SPD rule. 2023: 225.42 requires surge protection for outside feeders supplying dwelling units, dormitory units, hotel and motel guest rooms and suites, and nursing and limited-care patient sleeping rooms, mirroring 215.18 and 230.67.","r":"NEC 225.42 (2023)","q":"A new 2023 NEC rule, 225.42, requires what for outside feeders supplying dwelling units?","o":["Arc-flash labeling","Ground-fault protection of equipment","A neutral disconnect link","Surge protection"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC required SPDs only at dwelling unit services (230.67). The 2023 NEC added 215.18 for feeders and 225.42 for outside feeders, covering dwelling units, dormitory units, hotel and motel guest rooms and suites, and nursing home and limited-care patient sleeping rooms. Ground-fault protection (230.95), neutral disconnect links (250.30) and arc-flash labels (110.16) are different topics. Look in 225.42.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2020->2023","f":"Service surge protection expanded beyond dwelling units","b":"2020: 230.67 required an SPD only at services supplying dwelling units. 2023: it also applies to services supplying dormitory units, guest rooms and suites of hotels and motels, and areas of nursing homes and limited-care facilities used exclusively as patient sleeping rooms.","r":"NEC 230.67 (2023); 230.67 (2020)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, service SPD protection is required for services supplying dwelling units and also those supplying:","o":["Agricultural barns","Parking garages","Temporary construction trailers","Guest rooms and suites of hotels and motels"],"a":3,"e":"The 2023 NEC expanded the 2020 dwelling-only requirement to dormitory units, hotel and motel guest rooms/suites, and nursing home patient sleeping rooms. Look in 230.67(A).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2020->2023","f":"Minimum SPD rating for service surge protection","b":"2020: 230.67 required a Type 1 or Type 2 SPD with no minimum nominal discharge current. 2023: the SPD must have a nominal discharge current (In) rating of at least 10 kA.","r":"NEC 230.67 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, a service SPD installed under 230.67 must have a minimum nominal discharge current (In) rating of:","o":["10 kA","20 kA","5 kA","No minimum is specified"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC required a Type 1 or Type 2 SPD at dwelling unit services but gave no minimum discharge rating, which is why 'no minimum' is the tempting old answer. The 2023 NEC set a minimum nominal discharge current (In) of 10 kA. 5 kA and 20 kA are not the code values. Look in 230.67.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2020->2023","f":"Emergency disconnect 230.85 restructured: types, replacement, and nearby isolation","b":"2020: 230.85 required an outdoor emergency disconnect for one- and two-family dwelling services. 2023: the section is restructured with a list of acceptable disconnect types (service disconnect, meter disconnect, or other listed disconnect on the supply side), replacement of service equipment triggers the requirements, and nearby isolation equipment for other energy sources must be identified with a plaque or directory.","r":"NEC 230.85 (2023); 230.85 (2020)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 230.85, when service equipment is replaced at a one- or two-family dwelling:","o":["The emergency disconnect requirements apply to the replacement","Only the grounding changes","They do not apply to replacement work","Only a label must be added"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC introduced 230.85 emergency disconnects for services to one- and two-family dwellings but did not include a replacement trigger. The 2023 NEC restructured 230.85 to list acceptable disconnect types and to make the requirements apply when service equipment is replaced, plus identification (plaque or directory) of nearby isolation equipment for other energy sources. A replacement is not exempt, and it is more than a grounding change or a label. Look in 230.85.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2020->2023","f":"Service barriers: 'with the service disconnect in the open position'","b":"2020: 230.62(C) required barriers where energized parts could be exposed when servicing load terminations. 2023: the text adds the condition 'with the service disconnect in the open position' to clarify where barriers are required.","r":"NEC 230.62(C) (2023)","q":"Under 2023 NEC 230.62(C), barriers in service equipment protect against contact with energized line-side parts when:","o":["The panel is outdoors only","The service is over 1000 volts","The cover is installed","Servicing load terminations with the service disconnect in the open position"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 text of 230.62(C) required barriers so energized parts are not exposed while servicing load terminations but did not say under what condition. The 2023 NEC clarifies the condition: with the service disconnect in the open position. Covers on, outdoor-only and over-1000-volt limits are not part of the rule. Look in 230.62(C), and compare the new feeder rule in 215.15.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2020->2023","f":"Flexible bus systems added as a service-entrance wiring method","b":"2020: flexible bus systems were not a listed service-entrance wiring method in 230.43. 2023: 230.43 adds flexible bus systems (new Article 371) for service-entrance conductors of 1000 V or less.","r":"NEC 230.43, Article 371 (2023)","q":"Which wiring method was added to 230.43 for service-entrance conductors in the 2023 NEC?","o":["Flat conductor cable","Type NM cable","Open knob-and-tube wiring","Flexible bus systems"],"a":3,"e":"Article 371 (flexible bus systems) is new in 2023, and 230.43 now lists flexible bus systems as a permitted service-entrance wiring method for 1000 V or less. Knob-and-tube and flat conductor cable are not service wiring methods, and Type NM cable is never permitted for service entrance. Look in 230.43.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","step":"2020->2023","f":"14 AWG copper-clad aluminum small conductor protection","b":"2020: small-conductor limits in 240.4(D) covered copper and aluminum with specific sizes. 2023: new 240.4(D)(3) permits 14 AWG copper-clad aluminum with overcurrent protection not over 10 A (breakers or fuses listed and marked for it).","r":"NEC 240.4(D)(3) (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, 14 AWG copper-clad aluminum conductors may be protected at not more than:","o":["15 amperes","30 amperes","20 amperes","10 amperes"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no rule for 14 AWG copper-clad aluminum conductors in 240.4(D); 15 A is the limit for 14 AWG copper, which is the tempting wrong answer. The 2023 NEC added 240.4(D)(3) with a 10 A overcurrent limit for 14 AWG CCA, correlating with the new 10 A branch circuit in 210.23 and the 10 A standard rating in 240.6(A). 20 A and 30 A exceed what 14 AWG may carry. Look in 240.4(D)(3).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","step":"2020->2023","f":"10 A added as a standard overcurrent device rating","b":"2020: Table 240.6(A) standard ratings for breakers and fuses started at 15 A (with additional standard fuse ratings of 1, 3, 6, 10, and 601 A). 2023: 10 A is a standard rating in the table (for breakers and fuses).","r":"NEC 240.6(A) (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, the standard ampere ratings table 240.6(A) lists which new rating for both fuses and circuit breakers?","o":["10 amperes","12 amperes","18 amperes","5 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"In the 2020 NEC the standard ratings in 240.6(A) for circuit breakers started at 15 A, while fuses also had additional standard ratings of 1, 3, 6, 10 and 601 A. The 2023 NEC lists 10 A as a standard rating for both, supporting the new 10 A branch circuit in 210.23. 5 A, 12 A and 18 A are not standard ratings. Look in 240.6(A).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","step":"2020->2023","f":"New 240.7: listing of overcurrent and ground-fault devices","b":"2020: no single section requiring listing of these devices. 2023: 240.7 requires branch-circuit overcurrent protective devices, GFPE relays and breakers, and GFCI devices to be listed.","r":"NEC 240.7 (2023)","q":"A new 2023 NEC section 240.7 requires which devices to be listed?","o":["Only transformers","Branch-circuit overcurrent devices and GFCI devices","Only fuses","Only enclosures"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC did not have a single section requiring listing of these protective devices. The 2023 NEC added 240.7: branch-circuit overcurrent protective devices, ground-fault protection of equipment (GFPE) relays and breakers, and GFCI devices must be listed. It is not limited to fuses, transformers or enclosures. Look in 240.7.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","step":"2020->2023","f":"OCPD not permitted in any bathroom or shower area","b":"2020: overcurrent devices (other than supplementary) were not permitted in bathrooms of dwelling units, dormitories, and guest rooms or suites. 2023: not permitted in any bathroom, shower facility, or locker room with showering facilities.","r":"NEC 240.24(E) (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, overcurrent protective devices (other than supplementary) may not be installed in:","o":["Any bathroom, shower area, or locker room with showering facilities","Only hotel bathrooms","Only bathrooms of dwelling units","Only outdoor shower areas"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC prohibited overcurrent devices (other than supplementary) in the bathrooms of dwelling units, dormitories, and guest rooms or suites. The 2023 NEC broadens it to any bathroom, shower facility, or locker room with showering facilities, so it is not limited to dwellings, hotels, or outdoor showers. Look in 240.24(E).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 242 Overvoltage Protection","step":"2020->2023","f":"SPDs must indicate proper operation","b":"2020: Article 242 had no indicating requirement. 2023: 242.9 requires permanently connected SPDs rated 1000 V or less to provide a means to indicate they are functioning properly.","r":"NEC 242.9 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 242.9, an SPD must provide:","o":["An audible alarm only","An indication that it is functioning properly","A GFCI function","A built-in disconnect"],"a":1,"e":"Article 242 itself was created in the 2020 NEC, but it had no indicating requirement. The 2023 NEC added 242.9: permanently connected surge-protective devices of 1000 V or less must provide a means to indicate they are functioning properly (such as an indicator light). A built-in disconnect, audible alarm only, or GFCI function is not what the rule requires. Look in 242.9.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","step":"2020->2023","f":"GEC may not pass through enclosure ventilation openings","b":"2020: no rule addressing GECs passing through enclosure vent openings. 2023: new 250.64(G) prohibits routing a grounding electrode conductor through ventilation openings of an enclosure.","r":"NEC 250.64(G) (2023)","q":"Under 2023 NEC 250.64(G), a grounding electrode conductor shall not:","o":["Be bare","Be installed in PVC conduit","Be solid copper","Pass through ventilation openings of an enclosure"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC did not address grounding electrode conductors passing through enclosure ventilation openings. The 2023 NEC added 250.64(G), which prohibits a GEC from passing through a ventilation opening so airflow is not obstructed and the conductor is not exposed to heating. The conductor may still be solid copper, bare, or in PVC conduit as 250.64 allows. Look in 250.64(G).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","step":"2020->2023","f":"Plaster rings and extension rings in the ground-fault path","b":"2020: 250.109 covered metal enclosures connected by wiring methods. 2023: also names plaster (mud) rings and extension rings, which must be connected to the box to maintain an effective ground-fault path.","r":"NEC 250.109 (2023)","q":"Which items were specifically added to 250.109 in the 2023 NEC for maintaining the equipment grounding path?","o":["Wire nuts","Cable staples","Plaster rings and extension rings","Conduit straps only"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 text of 250.109 said metal enclosures could be connected together by wiring methods. The 2023 NEC explicitly names metal plaster rings and extension rings (and covers and fittings), which must be connected to the box to maintain an effective ground-fault path. Wire nuts, cable staples and conduit straps are not named. Look in 250.109.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","step":"2020->2023","f":"Chapter 3 wiring methods now cover up to 1500 V dc (300.2(A))","b":"2020: Chapter 3 wiring methods applied to systems of 1000 V nominal or less. 2023: 300.2(A) applies them to systems of 1000 V ac or 1500 V dc nominal or less; higher voltages are covered by the new over-1000 V articles such as 305.","r":"NEC 300.2(A) (2023); 300.2(A) (2020)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 300.2(A), Chapter 3 wiring methods are for systems of 1000 V ac or what maximum dc voltage, nominal?","o":["1500 V dc","2000 V dc","600 V dc","1000 V dc"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC stated a single 1000 V limit, so 1000 V dc is the tempting old answer. The 2023 NEC recognizes 1500 V dc to match PV and storage systems. 2000 V dc and 600 V dc are not limits in this section. Look in 300.2(A).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 310 Conductors for General Wiring","step":"2020->2023","f":"Dwelling service and feeder sizing relocated to 310.12","b":"2020: the permission to size single-phase dwelling unit service and feeder conductors (120/240 V, 100 A through 400 A) at 83 percent of the service rating was in 310.15(B)(7). 2023: it is relocated to new 310.12, 'Single-Phase Dwelling Services and Feeders,' with its own Table 310.12; 240.4(H) cross-references it.","r":"NEC 310.12 (2023); 310.15(B)(7) (2020)","q":"In the 2023 NEC, where is the 83 percent sizing permission for single-phase dwelling unit service and feeder conductors found?","o":["310.14(A)(3)","310.15(B)(7)","310.12","220.82"],"a":2,"e":"310.15(B)(7) is the 2020 location and is the tempting old answer. In the 2023 NEC the rule is in 310.12 with Table 310.12. 220.82 is the optional dwelling load calculation, a different topic. Look in 310.12 and 240.4(H).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 314 Boxes, Conduit Bodies, and Handhole Enclosures","step":"2020->2023","f":"New 314.5: screws and fasteners that enter boxes","b":"2020: no general rule limited field-installed screws or fasteners entering a box or fitting. 2023: new 314.5 requires machine-type screws with blunt ends (or protected ends) and limits how far they may extend into the wiring space so they cannot damage conductors; new 312.10 does the same for cabinets, cutout boxes and meter socket enclosures.","r":"NEC 314.5 (2023); 312.10 (2023)","q":"What hazard is addressed by the new 2023 NEC 314.5?","o":["Corroded box knockouts","Unsupported ceiling fan boxes","Overfilled boxes","Field-installed screws or fasteners that extend into the box wiring space and could damage conductors"],"a":3,"e":"The new section is about fasteners (such as long cover or mounting screws) projecting into the wiring space. Box fill is 314.16 and fan support is 314.27. Look in 314.5 for boxes and 312.10 for cabinets.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 334 Nonmetallic-Sheathed Cable","step":"2020->2023","f":"NM cable in multifamily dwelling detached garages","b":"2020: NM cable use in multifamily dwellings was limited and a detached garage serving a multifamily dwelling was not covered. 2023: 334.10(2) permits NM and NMC cable in multifamily dwellings and their detached garages of Types III, IV and V construction.","r":"NEC 334.10(2) (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 334.10, NM cable may be used in multifamily dwellings and their detached garages of which construction types?","o":["Types I and II","Types III, IV, and V","Type I only","Any construction type"],"a":1,"e":"Type NM cable is limited to buildings of Types III, IV and V construction (combustible). Types I and II are noncombustible. The 2020 NEC did not allow NM in detached garages of multifamily dwellings; the 2023 NEC does. Look in 334.10(2).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 352 Rigid Polyvinyl Chloride Conduit","step":"2020->2023","f":"PVC expansion fittings now address earth movement","b":"2020: 352.44 addressed only thermal expansion and contraction. 2023: 352.44 is split into (A) Thermal Expansion and Contraction and (B) Earth Movement, requiring expansion fittings where PVC emerges from the ground and settlement or frost heave can occur.","r":"NEC 352.44(B) (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 352.44(B), expansion fittings for rigid PVC conduit are also required where conduit is subject to:","o":["Earth movement, such as settling or frost heave","Vibration from motors","Lightning currents","Direct sunlight only"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC addressed only thermal expansion and contraction in 352.44. The 2023 NEC divided it into (A) thermal expansion and contraction and (B) earth movement, requiring expansion fittings where PVC conduit emerges from the ground and could be affected by settling or frost heave. Vibration, sunlight and lightning are not the listed conditions. Look in 352.44(B).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 369 Insulated Bus Pipe","step":"2020->2023","f":"New Article 369 (insulated bus pipe and tubular covered conductors)","b":"2020: no article for these products. 2023: new Article 369 covers listed insulated bus pipe (IBP) and tubular covered conductors (TCC).","r":"NEC Article 369 (2023)","q":"Which new 2023 NEC article covers insulated bus pipe and tubular covered conductors?","o":["Article 366","Article 369","Article 371","Article 376"],"a":1,"e":"Article 366 is auxiliary gutters, Article 371 is flexible bus systems and Article 376 is metal wireways. The 2020 NEC had no article for insulated bus pipe (IBP) or tubular covered conductors (TCC); the 2023 NEC added Article 369 for those listed products.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 371 Flexible Bus Systems","step":"2020->2023","f":"New Article 371 (flexible bus systems)","b":"2020: no article. 2023: Article 371 covers flexible bus systems, also recognized as a service-entrance wiring method in 230.43.","r":"NEC Article 371 (2023)","q":"Flexible bus systems are covered by which new article in the 2023 NEC?","o":["Article 372","Article 369","Article 368","Article 371"],"a":3,"e":"Article 368 is busways and Article 369 is insulated bus pipe. The 2020 NEC had no article for flexible bus systems; the 2023 NEC added Article 371, and 230.43 recognizes flexible bus systems as a service-entrance wiring method.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 406 Receptacles","step":"2020->2023","f":"Receptacle zone around tubs and showers: separated spaces excluded","b":"2020: 406.9(C) prohibited receptacles within a zone 3 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically from the tub rim or shower threshold, without regard to walls or doors. 2023: the zone does not include spaces separated from the tub or shower by a floor, wall, ceiling, room door, window or fixed barrier.","r":"NEC 406.9(C) (2023); 406.9(C) (2020)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 406.9(C), a receptacle within 3 ft of a tub but separated from it by a fixed wall is:","o":["Outside the restricted zone","Permitted only if GFCI-protected and 20 A","Permitted only if tamper-resistant and above 8 ft","Still prohibited"],"a":0,"e":"The 2023 change keeps the zone from reaching through walls and doors. A receptacle on the other side of a fixed barrier is outside the zone (other rules such as GFCI in 210.8 still apply). Look in 406.9(C).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 406 Receptacles","step":"2020->2023","f":"One receptacle in the tub/shower zone for bidets and electronic toilet seats","b":"2020: no exception; receptacles in the zone were prohibited. 2023: Exception No. 4 allows a single 120 V receptacle within the zone to supply an electronic toilet seat, bidet, or personal hygiene device, not located between the tub or shower and the toilet or bidet.","r":"NEC 406.9(C) Exception No. 4 (2023)","q":"Which appliance may be supplied by a single receptacle inside the tub/shower receptacle zone under the 2023 NEC exception?","o":["A portable heater","A clothes iron","An electronic bidet or toilet seat","A hair dryer"],"a":2,"e":"The exception is limited to electronic toilet seats, bidets and personal hygiene devices. It does not cover general bathroom appliances. Look in 406.9(C) Exception No. 4.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 408 Switchboards, Switchgear, and Panelboards","step":"2020->2023","f":"New 408.9: replacement panelboard interiors","b":"2020: no rule for installing a replacement panelboard interior (the 'guts') in an existing enclosure. 2023: new 408.9 provides requirements for replacement panelboard interiors installed in existing enclosures.","r":"NEC 408.9 (2023)","q":"A new 2023 NEC section, 408.9, covers which topic?","o":["Panelboard working clearances","Replacement panelboards (interiors) installed in existing enclosures","Switchboard bus bracing","Panelboard grounding bars"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no rule for replacing the interior of a panelboard in an existing enclosure. The 2023 NEC added 408.9 for replacement panelboard interiors. Working clearance is in 110.26, grounding is in 408.40, and bus bracing is a switchboard topic, so those are wrong choices. Look in 408.9.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 440 Air-Conditioning and Refrigerating Equipment","step":"2020->2023","f":"Disconnect doors that expose live parts","b":"2020: no requirement. 2023: where a disconnect door or cover exposes live parts in an area readily accessible to unqualified persons (for example residential spaces), the door must be lockable or require a tool to open.","r":"NEC 440.11 (2023)","q":"Under 2023 NEC 440.11, an AC disconnect enclosure door that exposes live parts and is readily accessible to unqualified persons must be:","o":["Painted yellow","Marked with the voltage only","Lockable or require a tool to open","Removable without tools"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no requirement about the door of an AC disconnect that exposes live parts. The 2023 NEC added that if a disconnect door or cover can expose live parts in a place readily accessible to unqualified persons, it must be lockable or require a tool to open. Paint color, being removable, or being vented does not address the hazard. Look in 440.11.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 512 Cannabis Oil Equipment","step":"2020->2023","f":"New Article 512","b":"2020: no article. 2023: new Article 512 covers cannabis oil extraction equipment and systems that use flammable materials in commercial and industrial facilities.","r":"NEC Article 512 (2023)","q":"A new 2023 NEC article, 512, covers:","o":["Cannabis oil equipment and systems using flammable materials","Aircraft hangars","Bulk storage plants","Spray application"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no cannabis oil article. The 2023 NEC added Article 512 for cannabis oil equipment and systems using flammable materials in commercial and industrial facilities. Aircraft hangars are Article 513, bulk storage plants 515 and spray application 516. Look in Article 512.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 517 Health Care Facilities","step":"2020->2023","f":"Reconditioned patient care equipment","b":"2020: the general reconditioned-equipment rules applied. 2023: new 517.6 says the NEC provisions for reconditioned equipment do not apply to patient care-related electrical equipment.","r":"NEC 517.6 (2023)","q":"According to the new 2023 NEC 517.6, reconditioned-equipment provisions do NOT apply to:","o":["Patient care-related electrical equipment","Luminaires in corridors","Panelboards in the facility","Receptacles in business offices"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC reconditioned-equipment rules applied broadly. The 2023 NEC added 517.6 stating that those provisions do not apply to patient care-related electrical equipment, which is regulated by other standards. Panelboards, luminaires and business-office receptacles are still covered by the NEC reconditioning rules. Look in 517.6.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 555 Marinas, Boatyards, and Docking Facilities","step":"2020->2023","f":"New 555.15: equipment replacement at docking facilities","b":"2020: none. 2023: modifications or replacement of equipment at docking facilities must comply with the NEC, the circuit must be inspected, and damaged equipment must be identified and repaired.","r":"NEC 555.15 (2023)","q":"A new 2023 NEC section, 555.15, addresses:","o":["Dock lighting color","Replacement of equipment at docking facilities","Boat lift motor sizing","Shore power cord length"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no rule about replacing equipment at docking facilities. The 2023 NEC added 555.15: modifications or replacements must comply with the NEC, the circuit must be inspected, and damaged existing equipment must be identified and repaired by a qualified person. It does not address boat lift motor sizing, dock light color, or cord length. Look in 555.15.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 300 General Requirements for Wiring Methods","step":"2020->2023","f":"Exit stairwell exterior lighting may be supplied from inside the enclosure","b":"2020: 300.25 said only wiring methods permitted in the exit enclosure could be installed there, and exterior egress lighting at the stairwell door was a problem to feed. 2023: a new exception permits exterior egress lighting at the stairwell exit to be powered from the circuit serving the stairwell lighting inside the enclosure.","r":"NEC 300.25 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 300.25, exterior egress lighting serving the exterior door of an exit stairwell enclosure may be:","o":["Supplied from the stairwell lighting circuit inside the exit enclosure","Supplied only from a circuit in a separate building","Omitted if the stairs are enclosed","Powered only by a battery"],"a":0,"e":"The new exception keeps the egress lighting on the same circuit as the interior stairwell lighting, so a loss of one does not leave the exit dark while the other is on. Check 300.25 and its exceptions.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","step":"2020->2023","f":"EVSE branch circuits: can multiple EVSE share a circuit in 2023?","b":"2020: each outlet or EVSE for EV charging had to be supplied by an individual branch circuit with no other outlets. 2023: one branch circuit may supply multiple EVSE when an energy management system controls the loads (625.42); an individual branch circuit is still required otherwise.","r":"NEC 625.40 (2023); 625.40 (2020)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 625.40, more than one EVSE may be supplied by a single branch circuit when:","o":["An energy management system controls the EVSE loads","All the EVSE are installed in the same garage","Each EVSE is rated 120 V","A GFCI breaker protects the circuit"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 rule was strictly one individual branch circuit per EVSE. The 2023 NEC allows sharing under an EMS (625.42, 750.30). Same garage, voltage and GFCI do not by themselves allow sharing. Look in 625.40.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 625 Electric Vehicle Power Transfer System","step":"2020->2023","f":"New 625.49: island-mode operation of EV power export","b":"2020: Article 625 covered bidirectional interactive systems in 625.48 but had no island-mode provision. 2023: new 625.49 allows bidirectional EVSE and electric vehicle power export equipment (EVPE) to be part of an interconnected power system that operates in island mode.","r":"NEC 625.49 (2023); 625.48 (2023)","q":"Which new 2023 NEC section permits bidirectional EVSE and EV power export equipment to be part of a system operating in island mode?","o":["625.101","625.52","625.17","625.49"],"a":3,"e":"625.48 covers interactive systems connected to the utility. The island-mode permission is new 625.49. 625.52 covers ventilation and 625.101 is wireless power transfer grounding. Look in 625.49.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 630 Electric Arc Welders","step":"2020->2023","f":"GFCI protection in welding work areas","b":"2020: Article 630 had no GFCI requirement. 2023: new 630.8 requires GFCI protection for personnel on 125 V, 15 and 20 A receptacles supplying hand tools or portable lighting in welding work areas (single-phase, 150 V or less to ground).","r":"NEC 630.8 (2023)","q":"A new 2023 NEC section, 630.8, requires GFCI protection for:","o":["The welder's output terminals","All welder disconnects","125 V, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles that supply hand tools or portable lighting","Only 480 V welders"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no GFCI requirement in Article 630. The 2023 NEC added 630.8: GFCI protection for personnel on 125 V, 15 and 20 A receptacles that supply hand tools or portable lighting in welding areas. The welder output and welder types are not what the rule covers. Look in 630.8.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","step":"2020->2023","f":"GFCI vs SPGFCI in Article 680","b":"2020: 680.5 required GFCI protection for pool equipment circuits without a separate device for higher voltages. 2023: 680.5 is split into (A) general, (B) GFCI for branch circuits of 150 V or less to ground, and (C) special purpose GFCI (SPGFCI) for circuits over 150 V to ground and not over 480 V.","r":"NEC 680.5 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 680.5, special purpose GFCI (SPGFCI) protection is used for pool equipment circuits rated:","o":["More than 50 volts to ground","More than 120 volts line to line","More than 150 volts to ground","Less than 50 volts"],"a":2,"e":"Class A GFCIs cover circuits at 150 V or less to ground. SPGFCIs cover higher-voltage circuits (up to 480 V) such as 240 V or 480 V pump motors. Look in 680.5(B) and (C).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","step":"2020->2023","f":"Pool definitions: the 42 inch depth test removed","b":"2020: a storable pool was defined by a maximum water depth of 42 in. (or nonmetallic, molded polymeric or inflatable walls). 2023: the 42 in. measurement is removed; a permanently installed pool is constructed in or partially in the ground (including all indoor pools) and a storable pool is entirely on or above the ground and designed to be stored and relocated.","r":"NEC Article 100 (2023); 680.2 (2020)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC definitions, a storable pool is one that is:","o":["Any pool under 1000 gallons","Less than 42 inches deep","Installed entirely on or above the ground and designed for storage and relocation","Any pool with a vinyl liner"],"a":2,"e":"Depth was part of the 2020 definition, which is the tempting wrong answer. In 2023 location (on or above the ground) and designed relocation define it. Look in Article 100.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","step":"2020->2023","f":"Pool heat pumps and chillers","b":"2020: 680.10 addressed electric pool water heaters, sized at 125 percent of the nameplate load. 2023: the section is expanded to include heat pumps and chillers, which must be listed for pool use and are sized from their nameplate data rather than the 125 percent heater rule.","r":"NEC 680.10 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 680.10, pool heat pumps and chillers must be:","o":["Sized at 125 percent of the heater load as for resistance heaters","Installed within 5 feet of the pool wall","Listed for pool use, with circuit sizing based on the equipment nameplate","Supplied by 15-ampere circuits only"],"a":2,"e":"Resistance heaters keep the 125 percent rule. Heat pumps and chillers follow nameplate minimum circuit ampacity and maximum overcurrent protection. Look in 680.10.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","step":"2020->2023","f":"Pool equipment rooms, pits and vaults: GFCI receptacle","b":"2020: 680.12 covered the maintenance disconnecting means and the receptacle rule for equipment rooms was in 680.22(A)(5). 2023: 680.12 is titled for equipment rooms, pits and vaults, requires drainage unless equipment is rated for submersion, and requires at least one GFCI-protected 125 V, 15 or 20 A receptacle in the equipment room.","r":"NEC 680.12 (2023)","q":"A pool equipment room must have at least one receptacle that is:","o":["Located inside the pool bonded grid","AFCI-protected","GFCI-protected, 125 V, 15 or 20 A","A 30-ampere twist-lock type"],"a":2,"e":"The receptacle requirement is for GFCI-protected 15 or 20 A receptacles. It was in 680.22 in 2020 and is now in 680.12 with drainage requirements. Look in 680.12.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 680 Swimming Pools","step":"2020->2023","f":"Fountain bonding conductor size","b":"2020: the bonding jumper for fountain equipment had no minimum size stated in the text. 2023: where a conductor is used for equipotential bonding of a fountain it must be solid copper not smaller than 8 AWG.","r":"NEC 680.54(B) (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 680.54(B), a conductor used for equipotential bonding of a fountain must be at least:","o":["12 AWG stranded copper","6 AWG aluminum","4 AWG insulated copper","8 AWG solid copper"],"a":3,"e":"The 2023 NEC states a minimum size for a conductor used for equipotential bonding of a fountain: 8 AWG solid copper. Stranded 12 AWG, aluminum and larger insulated conductors are not what the rule specifies. Look in 680.54(B).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","step":"2020->2023","f":"Floating PV systems","b":"2020: no specific provisions for PV equipment floating on water. 2023: floating PV (floatovoltaic) systems must use equipment identified and listed for the use, with wiring suitable for movement from wave action.","r":"NEC 690.4(G) (2023)","q":"A new 2023 NEC provision in 690.4 addresses:","o":["PV rooftop vent clearances","PV systems on aircraft","PV used only for pools","PV equipment floating on bodies of water"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no specific rules for floating PV. The 2023 NEC added floating PV system provisions to 690.4: equipment must be listed or identified for the environment (water and wave action) and wiring must be suitable for movement, including flexible or floating-rated wiring. PV on aircraft and pool-only PV are not what the section covers. Look in 690.4.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 690 Solar Photovoltaic Systems","step":"2020->2023","f":"Rapid shutdown exemption for detached, nonenclosed structures","b":"2020: PV on buildings, including carports and canopies attached to a building, was subject to rapid shutdown. 2023: new Exception No. 2 exempts detached, nonenclosed structures such as carports and canopies from the rapid shutdown requirements.","r":"NEC 690.12 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 690.12, rapid shutdown is not required for PV arrays on:","o":["Roofs with metal decking only","All residential roofs","All buildings over 2 stories","Detached nonenclosed structures such as carports or canopies"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC applied rapid shutdown to PV on or in buildings, including carports and canopies that are part of or attached to a building. The 2023 NEC added Exception No. 2 for detached, nonenclosed structures such as carports and canopies, but rooftop PV on enclosed buildings still needs rapid shutdown. Look in 690.12.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 691 Large-Scale Photovoltaic Electric Supply Stations","step":"2020->2023","f":"Arc-fault mitigation retitled fire mitigation","b":"2020: 691.10 was titled 'Arc-Fault Mitigation.' 2023: it is titled 'Fire Mitigation' with an added informational note.","r":"NEC 691.10 (2023)","q":"In the 2023 NEC, 691.10 for large-scale PV stations is titled:","o":["Ground-Fault Detection","Arc-Fault Mitigation","Fire Mitigation","Rapid Shutdown"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 NEC titled 691.10 'Arc-Fault Mitigation.' The 2023 NEC retitled it 'Fire Mitigation' and added an informational note, broadening it beyond arc faults. Rapid shutdown (690.12) and ground-fault detection (690.41) are in other sections. Look in 691.10.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","step":"2020->2023","f":"Emergency systems must be commissioned","b":"2020: emergency systems were tested and maintained after installation. 2023: 700.3 requires the emergency system to be commissioned (functionally tested and verified) as well as tested and maintained.","r":"NEC 700.3 (2023)","q":"In addition to testing and maintenance, the 2023 NEC 700.3 requires emergency systems to be:","o":["Operated only from utility power","Commissioned","Disconnected annually","Replaced every 10 years"],"a":1,"e":"The 2020 NEC required emergency systems to be tested and maintained. The 2023 NEC added commissioning in 700.3 (and 701.3 for legally required standby). Replacement every 10 years, annual disconnection, or utility-only operation are not requirements. Look in 700.3.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","step":"2020->2023","f":"Surge protection for emergency switchboards and panelboards","b":"2020: no surge protection requirement for emergency system equipment. 2023: 700.8 requires a surge-protective device in or on emergency system switchboards and panelboards.","r":"NEC 700.8 (2023)","q":"A new 2023 NEC 700.8 requires emergency system switchboards and panelboards to have:","o":["Arc-flash relays","A second main breaker","A surge-protective device","GFCI protection"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no surge protection rule for emergency system distribution equipment. The 2023 NEC added 700.8: a surge-protective device must be installed in or on emergency system switchboards and panelboards (switchgear). Arc-flash relays, a second main breaker or GFCI protection are not required. Look in 700.8.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 700 Emergency Systems","step":"2020->2023","f":"Selective coordination: replacement and modification","b":"2020: 700.32 required selective coordination of emergency system overcurrent devices. 2023: 700.32 is expanded: coordination must be selected by a qualified person and documented (A), re-evaluated when overcurrent devices are replaced (B) and when the system is modified (C); 701.32 and 708.54 are parallel.","r":"NEC 700.32 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 700.32, when an overcurrent device in an emergency system is replaced:","o":["The device must be oversized","No evaluation is needed","Selective coordination must be re-evaluated","Only a label is needed"],"a":2,"e":"The 2020 NEC required overcurrent devices in emergency systems to be selectively coordinated (700.32) but did not say what happens after changes. The 2023 NEC requires the coordination to be selected by a qualified person and documented, and to be re-evaluated when an overcurrent device is replaced or the system is modified; 701.32 and 708.54 are parallel. No evaluation, a label only, or oversizing the new device is wrong. Look in 700.32(B) and (C).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 705 Interconnected Electric Power Production Sources","step":"2020->2023","f":"New Part III for island-mode operation","b":"2020: no Part III in Article 705. 2023: new Part III covers interconnected systems operating in island mode, with sections on power source capacity, voltage and frequency control, and a single 120 V supply.","r":"NEC 705.80, 705.81, 705.82 (2023)","q":"A new Part III added to Article 705 in the 2023 NEC covers:","o":["Interconnected systems operating in island mode","Fuel cells only","Wind turbines only","Utility metering"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC Article 705 had Part I (General) and Part II (Microgrid Systems) only. The 2023 NEC added Part III for interconnected systems operating in island mode (705.80 power source capacity, 705.81 voltage and frequency control, 705.82 single 120 V supply). Utility metering, fuel cells only and wind turbines only are not the topics. Look in 705.80 through 705.82.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 706 Energy Storage Systems","step":"2020->2023","f":"ESS commissioning","b":"2020: no commissioning requirement. 2023: 706.7 requires commissioning of ESS before use, except in one- and two-family dwellings.","r":"NEC 706.7 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 706.7, commissioning of an energy storage system is NOT required at:","o":["One- and two-family dwellings","Hospitals","Factories","Schools"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no commissioning requirement for energy storage systems. The 2023 NEC divided 706.7 into (A) commissioning and (B) maintenance; commissioning is required before the ESS is placed in service except in one- and two-family dwellings. Hospitals, factories and schools are not exempt. Look in 706.7.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 710 Stand-Alone Systems","step":"2020->2023","f":"Stand-alone requirements moved from 690.10","b":"2020: stand-alone PV system rules were in 690.10. 2023: 690.10 was removed and the rules are in 710.15.","r":"NEC 710.15 (2023); 690.10 (2020)","q":"In the 2023 NEC, where are the requirements for stand-alone PV systems found?","o":["706.10","705.10","690.10","710.15"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC put the stand-alone PV system requirements in 690.10. The 2023 NEC removed 690.10 and placed the stand-alone rules in 710.15. 705.10 is the system directory and 706.10 is ESS scope. Look in 710.15.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 722 Cables for Power-Limited Circuits","step":"2020->2023","f":"Cable article for Class 2, 3, and 4","b":"2020: no article. 2023: Article 722 covers cables for power-limited and fault-managed power circuits.","r":"NEC Article 722 (2023)","q":"A new 2023 NEC article, 722, covers:","o":["Elevators","Fire pumps","Optical fiber only","Cables for power-limited and fault-managed power circuits"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 NEC spread power-limited and fire alarm cable requirements across Articles 725, 760 and 770. The 2023 NEC added Article 722 for cables for power-limited circuits (Class 2 and 3), power-limited fire alarm circuits and the new Class 4 fault-managed power circuits. Optical fiber is Article 770, fire pumps are 695 and elevators are 620. Look in Article 722.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 724 Class 1 Circuits","step":"2020->2023","f":"Class 1 circuits moved to new Article 724","b":"2020: Class 1 circuits were covered in Article 725. 2023: Class 1 circuits are in new Article 724, and Article 725 covers only Class 2 and Class 3.","r":"NEC Article 724, 725 (2023)","q":"In the 2023 NEC, Class 1 circuits are covered in:","o":["Article 726","Article 725","Article 724","Article 727"],"a":2,"e":"Article 725 covered Class 1 circuits in the 2020 NEC. In the 2023 NEC Class 1 circuits (limited to 30 V and 1000 VA when power-limited) are in new Article 724. Article 726 is Class 4 fault-managed power, and Article 727 is instrumentation tray cable. Look in Article 724.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 726 Class 4 Fault-Managed Power Systems","step":"2020->2023","f":"Class 4 fault-managed power systems","b":"2020: no article. 2023: new Article 726.","r":"NEC Article 726 (2023)","q":"Class 4 fault-managed power systems are covered by:","o":["Article 726","Article 722","Article 724","Article 725"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 NEC had no Class 4 article. The 2023 NEC added Article 726 for Class 4 fault-managed power systems, which detect faults and limit energy transfer. Article 725 is Class 2/3, 724 is Class 1 and 722 is the cable article. Look in Article 726.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 750 Energy Management Systems","step":"2020->2023","f":"EMS must be listed","b":"2020: no listing requirement. 2023: 750.6 requires energy management systems to be listed.","r":"NEC 750.6 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 750.6, an energy management system must be:","o":["Installed by a utility","Rated 600 volts","Installed outdoors","Listed"],"a":3,"e":"Article 750 (energy management systems) was new in the 2020 NEC but had no general listing rule. The 2023 NEC added 750.6, which requires energy management systems to be listed. Installation outdoors, a 600 volt rating, or utility installation are not requirements. Look in 750.6.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 530 Motion Picture and Television Studios","step":"2020->2023","f":"Article 530 rewritten for 2023","b":"2020: Article 530 had changed little for many years and used older studio terminology. 2023: the article was substantially rewritten to reflect current studio practice; it applies to studios in facilities and locations staffed by qualified persons, and Article 100 adds a definition of 'Location' (a place outside a studio where production is filmed or recorded).","r":"NEC Article 530, Article 100 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, which term was added to the Article 100 definitions to support the rewritten Article 530?","o":["Location, meaning a place outside a studio where production is filmed or recorded","Dimmer, meaning a switch that controls only incandescent lamps","Stage, meaning a studio floor under 1000 square feet","Portable, meaning anything under 20 pounds"],"a":0,"e":"The 2023 NEC rewrote Article 530 for motion picture and television production. The new definition of Location covers temporary production sites outside the studio. The other options are invented definitions. Look in Article 100 and Article 530.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 725 Class 1, 2 and 3 circuits","step":"2020->2023","f":"Article 725 narrowed to Class 2 and Class 3 circuits","b":"2020: Article 725 covered Class 1, Class 2 and Class 3 remote-control, signaling and power-limited circuits. 2023: Class 1 circuits moved to new Article 724, so Article 725 covers only Class 2 and Class 3 circuits (Class 4 fault-managed power is in new Article 726).","r":"NEC Article 725 (2023); Article 724 (2023)","q":"Which circuits does Article 725 cover in the 2023 NEC?","o":["Class 1, Class 2 and Class 3 circuits","Only fire alarm circuits","Class 2 and Class 3 remote-control, signaling, and power-limited circuits","Class 4 fault-managed power circuits only"],"a":2,"e":"In 2020 Article 725 also covered Class 1, which is the tempting old answer. In 2023 Class 1 is in Article 724 and Class 4 is in Article 726. Fire alarm circuits remain in Article 760.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","step":"2020->2023","f":"New 240.11: selective coordination of feeder overcurrent devices with the service device","b":"2020: where a code rule required a feeder overcurrent device to be selectively coordinated, the coordination requirement with the service device was not stated for the other feeders. 2023: new 240.11 says that if any feeder overcurrent device must be selectively coordinated with the service overcurrent device under another requirement, all feeder overcurrent devices supplied directly by that service overcurrent device must also be selectively coordinated with it.","r":"NEC 240.11 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC 240.11, if one feeder overcurrent device must be selectively coordinated with the service overcurrent device, then:","o":["Only feeders over 400 A need coordination","All feeder overcurrent devices supplied directly by the service overcurrent device must also be selectively coordinated with it","No other feeder devices need coordination","All branch circuit devices must be current-limiting"],"a":1,"e":"The new section prevents a non-coordinated feeder from tripping the service device. It applies when another section (such as 700.32 or 701.32) already requires coordination for one feeder. Look in 240.11.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2020->2023","f":"How is the 6 ft sink distance measured for GFCI in 2023?","b":"2020: the 6 ft distance to a sink was measured along the path of an appliance power-supply cord and the text included a clause about not passing through a window. 2023: the measurement applies to any power-supply cord plugged into the receptacle (the words 'of an appliance' were removed) and is the shortest path the cord would follow without piercing a floor, wall, ceiling or fixed barrier.","r":"NEC 210.8 (2023)","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, the 6 ft distance used for sink-area GFCI is measured along:","o":["The wall surface only","The shortest path the power-supply cord would follow without piercing a floor, wall, ceiling, or fixed barrier","A straight line through walls","The distance from the receptacle to the panelboard"],"a":1,"e":"The measurement is a cord path, not a straight line, and it cannot go through fixed barriers. Look in 210.8(A)(7) and 210.8(B) for the measurement language.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 110 Requirements for Electrical Installations","step":"2020->2023","f":"Interrupting rating wording: 'available fault current'","b":"2020: 110.9 required equipment interrupting ratings to be at least equal to the current available at the line terminals. 2023: the text says 'available fault current at the line terminals'; the requirement itself is unchanged.","r":"NEC 110.9 (2023)","q":"Under 2023 NEC 110.9, equipment intended to interrupt current at fault levels must have an interrupting rating at least equal to:","o":["125 percent of the continuous load","The ampere rating of the conductors","The available fault current at the line terminals","The full-load current of the load"],"a":2,"e":"The 2023 wording is a clarification to 'available fault current'. The technical requirement did not change. Look in 110.9.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 120 Branch-Circuit","step":"2023->2026","f":"Where did the NEC's branch-circuit, feeder and service load calculation rules move in the 2026 edition?","b":"2023 NEC: load calculations were in Article 220 (for example 220.82 optional dwelling calc). 2026 NEC: they were relocated to a new Article 120 with the same title, so 220.82 became 120.82 and 220.41 became 120.41.","r":"NEC 120.1 (2026); Art. 220 (2023)","q":"Under the 2026 NEC, in which article are branch-circuit, feeder and service load calculations found?","o":["Article 210","Article 130","Article 220","Article 120"],"a":3,"e":"In the 2023 NEC these rules were Article 220. The 2026 NEC moved them to Article 120 (Article 220 is no longer the load calculation article). Do not confuse it with Article 130, which is now energy management/power control systems. Per Texas municipal 2026 NEC significant-changes summaries, IAEI Magazine, EC&M and Electrical Contractor magazine. In the book, use the Article 120 table of contents and the Annex D examples.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 130 Energy Management Systems","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 NEC: which article now holds energy management system rules (formerly Article 750)?","b":"2023 NEC: Article 750 covered energy management systems. 2026 NEC: those rules moved to Article 130, and Article 130 gets a new Part II for power control systems (PCS). The number 750 is reused for limited-energy system grounding and bonding.","r":"NEC Art. 130 (2026); Art. 750 (2023)","q":"Under the 2026 NEC, energy management systems are covered in which article?","o":["Article 130","Article 750","Article 706","Article 120"],"a":0,"e":"Article 750 (2023) was relocated to Article 130 in 2026, per the Texas 2026 NEC significant-changes summary, IAEI Magazine, EC&M and Minnesota DLI's change summary. Article 750 still exists in 2026 but now deals with grounding and bonding of limited-energy systems (IAEI). Find it in the 2026 Chapter 1 table of contents.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 90 / Chapter 8","step":"2023->2026","f":"How did 90.3 change Chapter 8 (communications) in the 2026 NEC?","b":"2023 NEC: Chapter 8 was independent of Chapters 1-7 unless specifically referenced. 2026 NEC: Chapter 8's independence is removed, so Chapters 1-4 apply to communications systems unless modified, and Chapter 8 now addresses communications wires and cables outside and entering buildings.","r":"NEC 90.3 (2026); 90.3 (2023)","q":"Under the 2026 NEC, how does Chapter 8 relate to Chapters 1 through 4?","o":["Chapter 8 was deleted entirely","Chapter 8 is still independent of Chapters 1-7 unless specifically referenced","Chapter 8 is no longer independent; the general rules in Chapters 1-4 apply to it","Chapter 8 now overrides Chapters 1-4"],"a":2,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's 2026 change summary, IAEI Magazine, Belden and Electrical Contractor magazine, the independence of Chapter 8 was removed (described as among the biggest structural changes in 50+ years). Limited-energy content is consolidated in Chapter 7 (Arts. 720-760).","d":2},{"ar":"Global terminology","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 NEC global change: what replaced the phrase 'overcurrent protective device'?","b":"2023 NEC: 'overcurrent protective device' spelled out. 2026 NEC: the acronym OCPD is used throughout (700+ times), and 'AC' is added where rules apply only to alternating current.","r":"NEC global changes (2026)","q":"In the 2026 NEC, the term 'overcurrent protective device' is replaced throughout by:","o":["OPD","OCPD","OCD","BCOPD"],"a":1,"e":"Texas municipal summaries (over 700 uses) and EC&M's top-25 article both note the global OCPD change. EC&M also notes the older term 'overcurrent device' was updated.","d":1},{"ar":"Chapter 7 Limited-energy articles 720-723, 742, 750","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 limited-energy reorganization: what do new Articles 720, 721 and 722 cover?","b":"2026 NEC creates general limited-energy articles for Chapters 7/8: 720 (wiring methods and materials / installation), 721 (power sources for Class 2/3/4), 722 (cables), 723 (raceways, routing assemblies, trays), 742 (overvoltage protection) and 750 (grounding and bonding).","r":"NEC Arts. 720-723, 742, 750 (2026)","q":"In the 2026 NEC, which article covers limited-energy cables?","o":["Article 722","Article 721","Article 723","Article 720"],"a":0,"e":"EC&M, IAEI Magazine and Belden's article list the new limited-energy articles (Belden's list is pre-publication/provisional). Article 722 is limited-energy cable, 720 is installation/wiring methods, 721 is power sources and 723 is raceways, cable routing assemblies and trays.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 624 Electric Self-Propelled Vehicle Power Transfer","step":"2023->2026","f":"New 2026 Article 624: what does it cover?","b":"2023 NEC: no Article 624; all vehicle charging was in Article 625. 2026 NEC: new Article 624 covers power transfer systems for electric self-propelled vehicles that do not meet the Article 100 'Electric Vehicle' definition (forklifts, golf carts, electric boats, farm equipment, etc.). Article 625 still covers on-road electric vehicles.","r":"NEC Art. 624 (2026)","q":"Which 2026 NEC article covers power transfer for electric self-propelled vehicles such as forklifts and golf carts that are not 'electric vehicles' under Article 100?","o":["Article 625","Article 624","Article 691","Article 626"],"a":1,"e":"EC&M's list, IAEI Magazine's EV article, Minnesota DLI and the Mike Holt 2026 guide all identify new Article 624. Article 625 stays for on-road EV charging; Article 626 is electrified truck parking spaces.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions - limited energy","step":"2023->2026","f":"New 2026 definition: 'Cable, Limited-Energy' - what is it for?","b":"2023 NEC: no single term; rules relied on separate cable lists. 2026 NEC: Article 100 adds 'Cable, Limited-Energy' covering all limited-energy cable types (Class 2, 3 and 4 including fault-managed power, communications, CATV, optical fiber, fire alarm), and revises 'Limited-Energy System'.","r":"NEC Art. 100 (2026)","q":"The new Article 100 term 'Cable, Limited-Energy' in the 2026 NEC is intended to:","o":["Replace the definition of power-limited circuit cable only","Define medium-voltage cable","Cover all limited-energy cable types in one defined term, including Class 4 fault-managed power","Define only communications cable"],"a":2,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary, Belden's coverage and Electrical Contractor magazine (NECA article), the term was created largely because 'low-voltage' and 'power-limited' no longer describe Class 4 fault-managed power systems.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions - PCS / EMS","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 definitions: how do 'power control system (PCS)' and 'energy management system (EMS)' differ?","b":"2023 NEC: EMS defined as a system that monitors and/or controls; no PCS definition. 2026 NEC: EMS must both monitor and control loads. New defined term PCS (single-source or multisource) monitors and controls power and can prevent overloads, and is listed (UL 3141).","r":"NEC Art. 100, 120.7, 130.2 (2026)","q":"Under the 2026 NEC, which system is defined to monitor and control power so as to prevent overload conditions (for example limiting loads to protect a backup source)?","o":["Interactive inverter","Transfer equipment","Power control system (PCS)","Energy management system (EMS) in the 2023 sense"],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M's 'Key Revisions to Chapter 1' and Electrical Contractor magazine (NECA article), PCS is a new definition (EMS wording also changed), and 120.7 and Article 130 Part II were built around it. Solar/ESS articles note PCS listing to UL 3141.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions - GFCI classes","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 definitions: what are Class A, C, D and E in GFCI terminology?","b":"2023 NEC: GFCI and a special-purpose GFCI idea only. 2026 NEC: 'Class A' is added as an alternate term for a traditional GFCI (about 4-6 mA trip), and Class C, D and E special-purpose GFCIs (SPGFCI) are defined (UL 943C). Class C is used for listed HVAC equipment under 210.8(F).","r":"NEC Art. 100 (2026)","q":"In the 2026 NEC, a traditional 4-6 mA personnel-protection GFCI is also called a:","o":["Class C SPGFCI","Class E SPGFCI","Class D SPGFCI","Class A GFCI"],"a":3,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary, EC&M ('Key Revisions to Chapter 2'), IAEI Magazine and Interstates, the 2026 Article 100 adds Class A as an alternate term and defines Class C, D and E SPGFCIs. Class C is the one cited for HVAC under 210.8(F).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 100 Definitions - EV vs ESV","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 definitions: what separates an 'electric vehicle (EV)' from an 'electric self-propelled vehicle (ESV)'?","b":"2023 NEC: only 'electric vehicle' was defined. 2026 NEC: EV means on-road automotive-type vehicles (including plug-in hybrids), and ESV covers other self-propelled electric vehicles (farm equipment, boats, aircraft, golf carts, forklifts). EVs go to Article 625; ESVs to new Article 624.","r":"NEC Art. 100, 624, 625 (2026)","q":"A battery-electric golf cart or forklift is treated under the 2026 NEC as:","o":["An electric self-propelled vehicle covered by Article 624","An electric vehicle covered by Article 625","Covered by Article 480 only","Outside the scope of the NEC"],"a":0,"e":"IAEI Magazine's EV infrastructure article (EV vs ESV definitions) and Minnesota DLI's change summary (Article 624 is for vehicles not meeting the Article 100 EV definition) both support this. EC&M adds that Article 624 includes forklifts, golf carts and electric boats.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 110 General Requirements","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 110.3(B): what happens when manufacturer instructions conflict with the NEC?","b":"2023 NEC: it was unclear whether listing/labeling instructions or NEC rules controlled. 2026 NEC: instructions must result in an installation and use that complies with the NEC. They can add requirements but cannot reduce them (for example they cannot waive a GFCI requirement). Instructions may be printed, a QR code or a link.","r":"NEC 110.3(B) (2026); 110.3(B) (2023)","q":"Under 2026 110.3(B), if manufacturer installation instructions would allow something the NEC prohibits:","o":["The AHJ must approve the instructions case by case","Both are optional for listed equipment","The instructions govern because the product is listed","The NEC still governs; instructions cannot make an installation less stringent than the Code"],"a":3,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, Minnesota DLI (which gives GFCI as an example), the Texas summary and Electrical Contractor magazine, a sentence was added requiring instructions to yield NEC-compliant installation and use.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 110 General Requirements","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 110.26: at what door position are equipment doors judged against the working-space egress path?","b":"2023 NEC: wording about doors was less specific. 2026 NEC: the egress path is evaluated with equipment doors at 90 degrees, even if the doors can open further or be removed; the required egress is at least 24 in. wide and 6.5 ft high. Language was also added for enclosed live parts.","r":"NEC 110.26 (2026); 110.26 (2023)","q":"Under the 2026 NEC, equipment doors are checked for obstructing the working space egress path with the doors open:","o":["120 degrees","45 degrees","90 degrees","180 degrees"],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M's 'Key Revisions to Chapter 1', Electrical Contractor magazine (NECA article), Interstates and the Texas summary, doors are measured at 90 degrees. Minnesota DLI describes it as assessing door obstruction regardless of position, removability or swing angle. Egress dimensions 24 in. wide and 6.5 ft high come from the NECA article.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 110 General Requirements","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 110.26(C)(2) large equipment: does the entrance/egress rule now cover feeder disconnects?","b":"2023 NEC: the large-equipment access/egress rule referred to service disconnecting means (1200 A or more, over 6 ft wide). 2026 NEC: it extends to service and feeder disconnecting means, with a reference to 225.33(A) for feeder disconnects in a single location.","r":"NEC 110.26(C)(2) (2026); 110.26(C)(2) (2023)","q":"The 2026 110.26(C)(2) large-equipment entrance and egress rule now also applies to:","o":["Only service disconnects, as before","Dwelling unit panelboards","Feeder disconnecting means (see 225.33(A)) in addition to service disconnects","Only generator disconnects"],"a":2,"e":"Per Electrical Contractor magazine (NECA article), Minnesota DLI's change summary, Interstates and the Texas summary. The NECA article states the 1200 A / 6 ft thresholds for the prior rule.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 120 Load Calculations","step":"2023->2026","f":"New 2026 120.13: what is the 3 VA per square foot figure still used for?","b":"2023 NEC: 3 VA/sq ft drove both load calculations and the number of lighting/receptacle circuits (210.11(A)). 2026 NEC: 120.13 keeps 3 VA/sq ft only to determine the minimum number of dwelling general lighting and receptacle branch circuits; it is not used for the service/feeder calculation, which uses 2 VA/sq ft.","r":"NEC 120.13 (2026); 220.14/220.41 (2023)","q":"In the 2026 NEC, 3 VA per square foot is retained in 120.13 for:","o":["Determining the minimum number of general lighting and receptacle branch circuits in a dwelling","Sizing the service conductors","The optional method feeder calculation","Sizing the grounding electrode conductor"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M (dwelling unit calculations article), Kopperfield, Electrical Contractor magazine (NECA article) and the Mike Holt study guide, 120.13 prevents the circuit count from falling when the service load value dropped to 2 VA. Example: 5000 sq ft x 3 VA = 15,000 VA, 125 A at 120 V, so 9 15-A or 7 20-A circuits.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 120 Load Calculations","step":"2023->2026","f":"EVSE in the optional dwelling load calculation: what percentage?","b":"2026 NEC: 120.82(D) takes EVSE at 100 percent of the nameplate rating with no demand factor, per 120.57.","r":"NEC 120.82(D) (2026)","q":"In the 2026 optional method for dwellings, EVSE load is counted at:","o":["50 percent of nameplate","100 percent of EVSE nameplate rating, no demand factor","125 percent of nameplate","80 percent of the branch-circuit rating"],"a":1,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, LightNow's article and the Mike Holt study guide (EVSE at 100 percent of nameplate). Do not confuse with the 80 percent figure used for EVSE added to existing-dwelling calculations in 120.83.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 130 Power Control Systems","step":"2023->2026","f":"Article 130 in 2026: what is new Part II?","b":"2023 NEC: Article 750 covered EMS only. 2026 NEC: Article 130 contains EMS rules (Part I, with all equipment listed) and a new Part II (130.50-130.80) for power control systems that can control generation, storage, loads and circuit controllers to prevent overload.","r":"NEC Art. 130, 130.2, 130.50 (2026)","q":"In the 2026 NEC, Article 130 Part II covers:","o":["Fire pump controllers","Power control systems (PCS)","Optional standby systems","Overvoltage protection"],"a":1,"e":"EC&M's top-25 article, Minnesota DLI's change summary and Electrical Contractor magazine (NECA article) describe Article 130 Part II and note EMS/PCS must be listed (informational notes reference UL 916 and UL 3141).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 200 Use and Identification of Grounded Conductors","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 200.7(A)(9): new way to identify a small grounded conductor?","b":"2023 NEC: grounded conductors of 6 AWG or smaller were identified by white or gray, or three continuous white/gray stripes. 2026 NEC: a new item allows a single continuous non-green stripe to identify the grounded conductor (6 AWG and smaller).","r":"NEC 200.7(A)(9) (2026)","q":"Under the 2026 NEC, a 6 AWG or smaller grounded conductor may be identified by:","o":["A single non-green stripe, in addition to the previously recognized methods","Only a green stripe","A tag at both ends only","A red stripe only"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M ('Key Revisions to Chapter 2'), Minnesota DLI's change summary and a 2026 training quiz (stripe or other marking for 6 AWG and smaller). Check 200.7 in the 2026 book for exact placement.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 HVAC outlets and GFCI: what is the new Class C option?","b":"2023 NEC: listed HVAC equipment outlets had a temporary exception from outdoor GFCI. 2026 NEC: Exception No. 3 permits a listed Class C SPGFCI to protect listed HVAC equipment, and the HVAC disconnect must be marked 'Class C SPGFCI protection is provided for an HVAC unit' (wording per Minnesota DLI). Non-readily-accessible outlets and outdoor lighting outlets are not covered by 210.8(F).","r":"NEC 210.8(F) Ex. 3 (2026); 210.8(F) Ex. (2023)","q":"In the 2026 NEC, an outdoor listed HVAC unit at a dwelling may be protected by:","o":["No ground-fault protection is ever allowed","Any AFCI breaker","A Class C SPGFCI, with a warning marking at the disconnect","Only a Class A GFCI"],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2' (Exception 3 new), Minnesota DLI's FAQ and change summary (marking text), IAEI Magazine and EC&M's top-25 article (Sept. 1, 2026 date on the older exception). Timing of the old exception differs among sources, so read the exception text in your adopted edition.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 210.8: what do 'HF' and 'HF+' markings on GFCIs mean?","b":"2023 NEC: no mention. 2026 NEC: an informational note recognizes Class A GFCIs marked HF or HF+ that tolerate high-frequency ground-fault currents, which matter for inverter-driven equipment such as variable-speed HVAC.","r":"NEC 210.8 Info. Note (2026)","q":"In the 2026 NEC, a GFCI marked 'HF' is intended to address:","o":["Weather resistance","Arc faults","High-frequency ground-fault current from inverter-driven equipment","Higher voltage ratings"],"a":2,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary, IAEI Magazine, EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2' and EC&M's Chapter 2 coverage (two new informational notes about GFCI device changes).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 210.8(A): what changed for GFCI in garages and accessory buildings?","b":"2023 NEC: one item covered garages and accessory buildings that have a floor at or below grade level. 2026 NEC: garages are a separate item (2); accessory buildings not intended as habitable rooms and limited to storage, work and similar uses are item (3), and the 'floor at or below grade' qualifier was removed.","r":"NEC 210.8(A)(2), (A)(3) (2026); 210.8(A)(2) (2023)","q":"Under the 2026 NEC, GFCI protection for accessory buildings (storage/work use) at a dwelling applies:","o":["Only where the floor is at or below grade","Only if the building has a bathroom","Regardless of whether the floor is at or below grade","Only for 20 A receptacles above 6 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's FAQ and a 2026 NEC course outline listing 210.8(A)(3) for accessory buildings. Minnesota explains elevation does not remove the shock hazard. Habitable areas in or above a garage are treated the same as accessory building habitable areas.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 new 210.52(C)(4): what is it?","b":"2023 NEC: no such list of prohibited locations is described by the sources. 2026 NEC: new 210.52(C)(4) is a list of locations where receptacles are prohibited, including within 24 in. of the countertop surface (such as on walls extending from base cabinets).","r":"NEC 210.52(C)(4) (2026)","q":"New 210.52(C)(4) in the 2026 NEC addresses:","o":["Spacing of island receptacles","Where to add a GFCI","AFCI exceptions","Locations where countertop receptacle outlets are prohibited, including within 24 in. of the countertop"],"a":3,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article (item 11), Minnesota DLI's change summary (new subdivision) and its FAQ (adjacent walls extending from base cabinets, measured from the nearest surface edge).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 lighting in a closet laundry area: new exception?","b":"2023 NEC: a switch-controlled lighting outlet was required in laundry areas. 2026 NEC: Exception No. 3 allows a lighting outlet outside the laundry area to provide illumination for a laundry located in a closet.","r":"NEC 210.70(A)(1) Ex. 3 (2026)","q":"Under 2026 210.70(A)(1) Exception No. 3, the lighting for a closet-type laundry area may be provided by:","o":["A lighting outlet located outside the laundry area","A receptacle-controlled lamp only","No lighting at all","A battery lantern"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary and a 2026 training quiz (closet laundry light/control changes) and the 2026 NEC course outlines listing 210.70(A)(1).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 210 Branch Circuits","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 210.17: which additional occupancy is covered for guest rooms/suites with permanent cooking?","b":"2023 NEC: hotels, motels, assisted living and similar guest rooms and suites with permanent cooking provisions had to meet dwelling unit branch-circuit rules. 2026 NEC: dormitories are added.","r":"NEC 210.17 (2026)","q":"Under the 2026 NEC, dormitory guest rooms/suites with permanent provisions for cooking are:","o":["Exempt from AFCI and GFCI requirements","Required to use 15 A circuits only","Covered only by Article 517","Covered by the same branch-circuit requirements as hotel and motel guest rooms with cooking"],"a":3,"e":"Per EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2' (dormitories added) and a 2026 training quiz (rules now apply to dormitories and break rooms with permanent cooking). Detail on exact lists should be read in 210.17.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 215 / 230 Surge protection","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 surge protection: what occupancy was added to 215.18 and 230.67?","b":"2023 NEC: SPD required for dwelling units, dormitories, hotel/motel guest rooms and suites, and nursing home/limited-care patient sleeping rooms. 2026 NEC adds sleeping quarters in fire, police, ambulance, rescue and ranger stations (230.67(A)), and extends to load-side feeder panelboards (215.18). Type 1 or Type 2 SPD with In of at least 10 kA.","r":"NEC 215.18, 230.67(A) (2026)","q":"Which occupancy is newly added to the 2026 surge-protection requirements for sleeping quarters?","o":["Parking garages","Sleeping quarters in fire, police, ambulance, rescue and ranger stations","Warehouses","Retail stores"],"a":1,"e":"Per EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2' (230.67(A) item 5 new), RectorSeal's 2026 SPD article, the Texas summary (emergency responder sleeping areas) and Minnesota DLI's FAQ (10 kA In, Type 1 or 2).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 225 Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 outside feeder disconnect (225.31): where must it be?","b":"2023 NEC: feeder disconnect on or inside the building, inside at nearest point of entrance. 2026 NEC: an outdoor feeder disconnect must be readily accessible and on or within sight of the building (110.29; within sight means visible and not more than 50 ft); inside, at the nearest point of entrance. A single disconnect may satisfy 225.31 and 225.41.","r":"NEC 225.31(A), (B), 225.41 (2026)","q":"For an outside feeder disconnect serving a dwelling in the 2026 NEC, the disconnect must be readily accessible and:","o":["On or within sight of the building served","At the utility pole only","Inside the first floor only","Anywhere on the property"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's FAQ and change summary, Electrical Contractor magazine (Chapter 2 coverage; 225.31 'within sight' approach) and an ecmag dwelling emergency disconnect article (225.41, 110.29). Within sight is the Article 100 definition (50 ft).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 service disconnect for one- and two-family dwellings: where must it be?","b":"2023 NEC: the service disconnect could be inside the dwelling nearest the point of entry, and an outdoor emergency disconnect was separately required (230.85). 2026 NEC: the service disconnect must be in a readily accessible outdoor location on or within sight of the dwelling (110.29). An exception allows a remote disconnect if an emergency disconnect is installed under 225.41.","r":"NEC 230.70(A)(1) (2026); 230.70, 230.85 (2023)","q":"Under the 2026 NEC, the service disconnecting means for a one- or two-family dwelling must be located:","o":["Nearest the point of entrance inside the dwelling","At the meter only if the utility requires","In a readily accessible outdoor location on or within sight of the dwelling","Inside the garage only"],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2', Minnesota DLI, IAEI Magazine and Electrical Contractor magazine. This effectively folds the 230.85 emergency disconnect into the service disconnect rule.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2023->2026","f":"What happened to 230.85 Emergency Disconnects in the 2026 NEC?","b":"2023 NEC: 230.85 required an outdoor emergency disconnect for one- and two-family dwellings. 2026 NEC: 230.85 is deleted and its content moved into 230.70 (disconnect location, markings, plaque).","r":"NEC 230.70 (2026); 230.85 (2023)","q":"In the 2026 NEC, the emergency disconnect requirements for one- and two-family dwellings are found in:","o":["225.42","230.95","230.85 unchanged","230.70 (230.85 was deleted)"],"a":3,"e":"EC&M's top-25 article, EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2', Minnesota DLI and Electrical Contractor magazine note 230.85 was removed and merged into 230.70. A utility disconnect no longer counts as the emergency disconnect unless it complies (Minnesota DLI).","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 marking for a dwelling emergency disconnect: what do the letters look like?","b":"2023 NEC: marking rules lived in 230.85. 2026 NEC: 230.70(B)(2) requires 'EMERGENCY DISCONNECT' on the front of the enclosure, white letters on a red background, at least 1/2 in. high, durable and not hand-written (110.21(B)). Non-dwelling service disconnects are marked 'SERVICE DISCONNECT'.","r":"NEC 230.70(B) (2026); 230.85 (2023)","q":"Under the 2026 NEC, the enclosure marking for a one- and two-family dwelling emergency disconnect must be:","o":["Any legible handwritten label","Black text on yellow, 1/4 in.","White text on a red background, letters at least 1/2 in. high","Red text on white, 1 in."],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article and EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2' (both give red background, white text, 1/2 in.), citing 230.70(B). Both attribute the content to the relocated 230.85.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 230.70(D): what has to be at the service disconnect for other energy sources?","b":"2023 NEC: only dwelling emergency disconnects had to be identified by a plaque or directory. 2026 NEC: any isolation disconnect for other energy sources (PV, storage, generators) not located at the service disconnect must be identified by a plaque or directory at the service disconnect.","r":"NEC 230.70(D) (2026); 230.85 (2023)","q":"In the 2026 NEC, a plaque or directory at the service disconnect is required to identify:","o":["The utility meter number","The ground resistance of the electrode","The location of disconnects for other energy sources not at the service disconnect","The date of the load calculation"],"a":2,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary, EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2' (applied only to dwelling emergency disconnects in 2023), EC&M's top-25 article, the Texas summary and Interstates.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 230 Services","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026: can a shunt-trip pushbutton serve as the service disconnect?","b":"2026 NEC: 230.70(F) says a remote-control device such as a shunt-trip pushbutton may actuate the disconnect but is not itself the service disconnecting means.","r":"NEC 230.70(F) (2026)","q":"Under 2026 230.70(F), a remote-control device that trips the service disconnect:","o":["Is prohibited entirely","Is required for all dwellings","Qualifies as the service disconnecting means","May operate the disconnect but does not itself qualify as the service disconnecting means"],"a":3,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article and EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 2'.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 240 Overcurrent Protection","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 240.24(E): bathroom panelboards - what exception was added?","b":"2023 NEC: overcurrent devices were not permitted in bathrooms. 2026 NEC: an exception lets OCPDs be added to an existing bathroom panelboard that was installed under prior editions.","r":"NEC 240.24(E) (2026); 240.24(E) (2023)","q":"The new 2026 exception in 240.24(E) allows:","o":["Adding OCPDs to an existing panelboard in a bathroom installed under prior editions","New panelboards in any bathroom","Subpanels in tub areas","Panelboards in showers"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary and a 2026 NEC course topic list (where OCPDs are permitted, including in bathrooms). The general bathroom prohibition remains.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 250 Grounding and Bonding","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 grounding electrode conductor splices: new allowance?","b":"2023 NEC: a GEC had to be one continuous length without a splice except irreversible compression-type connectors or exothermic welding, and busbars. 2026 NEC: splices are also permitted using listed grounding and bonding equipment at accessible locations.","r":"NEC 250.64(C) (2026); 250.64(C) (2023)","q":"In the 2026 NEC, a grounding electrode conductor may be spliced using:","o":["Listed grounding and bonding equipment at an accessible location (in addition to exothermic welding or irreversible compression)","Any approved tape","Wire nuts","Twist-on splice caps"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI (FAQ and summary), the Texas summary and a Mike Holt course topic list (permitted splicing methods for GEC). Exothermic welding and irreversible compression connectors stay permitted.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 300 Wiring Methods","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 300.4(C): what must be done with conductors damaged by fire, heat, water or corrosion?","b":"2023 NEC: reconditioning rules (110.20) did not clearly address wiring methods and conductors. 2026 NEC: conductors and wiring methods made unsuitable by overheating, fire, water or corrosion must be replaced. Informational notes cite NEMA GD 1-2019 (water-damaged equipment) and GD 2-2021 (fire/heat-damaged).","r":"NEC 300.4(C) (2026)","q":"Under 2026 300.4(C), conductors and wiring methods damaged by fire or water exposure must be:","o":["Painted","Replaced","Cleaned and reused","Re-tested with a megohmmeter and reused"],"a":1,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 3', Minnesota DLI and the Texas summary. NEMA GD 1-2019 is for water damage and GD 2-2021 for fire/heat.","d":1},{"ar":"Art. 300 Wiring Methods","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 cable ties: what do they have to be?","b":"2023 NEC: cable-tie listing was addressed in individual wiring-method articles. 2026 NEC: 300.13(E) says cable ties used to secure cable, flexible conduit or flexible tubing must be listed and identified for that purpose (the same for fixing devices), including Types 2S and 21S.","r":"NEC 300.13(E) (2026)","q":"Under the 2026 300.13(E), cable ties used to secure cables must be:","o":["Only metal ties","Listed and identified for the purpose","Any plastic tie rated for the temperature","Approved by the AHJ only"],"a":1,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, Minnesota DLI's change summary (listed and identified for securement and support) and EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 3'.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 312 Cabinets and Cutout Boxes","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 312.11: can service conductors pass through non-service enclosures?","b":"2023 NEC: 230.7 kept service conductors out of raceways with non-service conductors, but no enclosure-level rule existed. 2026 NEC: service conductors may not pass through enclosures containing switches or OCPDs that are not service equipment. Splices and pass-through are still allowed inside service equipment. CT and meter socket enclosures are not covered.","r":"NEC 312.11 (2026)","q":"Under the 2026 NEC, service conductors may NOT pass through:","o":["A CT cabinet","Service equipment enclosures","A listed meter socket enclosure","An enclosure containing switches or overcurrent devices that is not service equipment"],"a":3,"e":"Per EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 3' (312.11) and the Mike Holt 2026 course topic list (312.8, 312.11).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 314 Outlet Boxes","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 ceiling fan boxes: which locations require fan-rated boxes?","b":"2023 NEC: nearly every ceiling outlet box in habitable dwelling space had to be listed for fan support. 2026 NEC: the fan-box requirement applies to ceiling outlet boxes in sleeping rooms, living rooms and designated or typical fan locations; boxes must be listed for fan support or allow access to framing to support a fan without removing the box.","r":"NEC 314.27(B) (2026); 314.27 (2023)","q":"Under 2026 314.27(B), fan-rated ceiling outlet boxes are required in dwelling:","o":["Living and sleeping areas at typical or designated fan locations","Garages only","Every room including kitchen pendant locations","Bathrooms only"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 3' and Minnesota DLI's FAQ. (EC&M describes the 2023 rule as covering nearly all habitable spaces.) Verify the section location in the 2026 book.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 320/330/334 Cable securing","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026: must straps, staples and hangers for NM, AC and MC cable be listed?","b":"2023 NEC: NM cable ties had to be listed and identified, but hangers, staples and straps had no listing requirement. 2026 NEC: hangers, staples, straps and cable ties used to secure and support NM cable (334.2, 334.30), and similar Types AC (320.2) and MC (330.2), must be listed. No 'other approved' option remains in 334.30.","r":"NEC 334.2, 334.30, 320.2, 330.2 (2026)","q":"Under the 2026 NEC, supports such as staples and straps used for NM cable must be:","o":["Approved by the manufacturer only","Any metal strap","Listed","Unlisted if painted"],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M 'Key Revisions to Chapter 3' (334.2), NFPA's first-revision record (320.30 listed ties, staples, straps, hangers) and a 2026 training quiz (320.2, 332.3, 334.2 listed supports; MI staples removed).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 334 NM Cable","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 NM cable in small storage buildings: thermal barrier exception?","b":"2023 NEC: NM cable in buildings where it is permitted had to be protected by a 15-minute finish rating thermal barrier where required. 2026 NEC: an exception permits NM without the 15-minute thermal barrier in non-habitable, grade-level storage garages and storage buildings under 1500 sq ft (total building area).","r":"NEC 334.10 Ex. to (3) (2026)","q":"Under the 2026 334.10 exception, NM cable need not have the 15-minute thermal barrier in:","o":["Non-habitable, grade-level storage buildings under 1500 sq ft","Any dwelling over 1500 sq ft","Multifamily buildings","Habitable accessory dwelling units"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary and FAQ (area counts the whole building, not individual storage units).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 408 Switchboards and Panelboards","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 408.6: what documentation of available fault current is required?","b":"2023 NEC: SCCR of switchboards, switchgear and panelboards not less than the available fault current. 2026 NEC: also requires, for other than dwellings, that available fault current and the calculation date be field marked at the point of supply, documented, recalculated and re-marked after modifications, and replacement OCPDs must have interrupting ratings at least equal to the marked value.","r":"NEC 408.6 (2026); 408.6 (2023)","q":"Under 2026 408.6, after a modification that changes available fault current, a non-dwelling installation must:","o":["Replace the panelboard","Do nothing","Recalculate the available fault current and update the field marking","Mark only the voltage"],"a":2,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article (408.6 details), a 2026 NEC course outline and the Mike Holt course list (408.6, 110.10). Details are from one detailed source; confirm exact wording at 408.6.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 430 Motors","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 motor control center marking: what must be marked?","b":"2026 NEC: where an MCC is supplied by a feeder, it must be marked with the name and location of the means required to disconnect all power to it.","r":"NEC 430.98(A) (2026)","q":"Under 2026 430.98(A), an MCC supplied by a feeder must be marked with:","o":["The location of the means to disconnect all power to the MCC","The motor horsepower of the largest load","The date of manufacture","The arc-flash PPE category only"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary and Interstates' 430.98 coverage.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 440 HVAC","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 new 440.15: what marking is required?","b":"2023 NEC: no such section. 2026 NEC: for non-dwelling installations, a split-system exterior disconnect must have marking identifying the indoor units it supplies (indoor-unit markings at the disconnect).","r":"NEC 440.15 (2026)","q":"New 440.15 in the 2026 NEC requires, for a split-system outdoor disconnect in other than one- and two-family dwellings:","o":["Arc-flash labeling only","A GFCI receptacle","Marking that identifies the indoor units supplied","A red disconnect cover"],"a":2,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary, a 2026 training quiz (outdoor disconnect marking for split systems in non-dwellings) and the Mike Holt course list (440.15).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 480 Stationary Batteries","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 Article 480: what changed in scope and charging?","b":"2023 NEC: Article 480 applied to stationary standby battery installations. 2026 NEC: 'standby' was removed so it covers all stationary batteries not listed as an energy storage system (Informational Note), and 480.14 requires overcharge prevention for stationary battery systems.","r":"NEC 480.1, 480.14 (2026)","q":"Under the 2026 NEC, Article 480 applies to:","o":["All stationary battery installations (not only standby), with overcharge prevention required","Only lead-acid batteries","Only batteries over 1000 V","Only standby batteries"],"a":0,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary (480.1 note, 480.14 overcharge) and a 2026 training quiz (word 'standby' removed; stationary batteries need overcharge control).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 500 Hazardous Locations","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026: where did bonding rules for hazardous locations move?","b":"2023 NEC: bonding requirements for Class I, II and III were in Articles 501, 502 and 503. 2026 NEC: they are consolidated into new 500.30.","r":"NEC 500.30 (2026); 501.30, 502.30, 503.30 (2023)","q":"In the 2026 NEC, bonding requirements for hazardous (classified) locations are consolidated in:","o":["505.25","250.100","500.30","501.30 only"],"a":2,"e":"Per Interstates, IAEI Magazine's proposed-changes article (new 500.30 for Arts. 501-503) and a 2026 training quiz (500.30).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 501-506 Hazardous Locations wiring methods","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026: how are wiring methods presented in hazardous location articles?","b":"2023 NEC: wiring methods listed in text. 2026 NEC: wiring methods consolidated into tables (e.g., Tables 501.10(A)(1) and (B)(1)); six methods were added: MC-HL, ITC-HL, TC-ER-HL, Type P cable, RMC and PVC conduit.","r":"NEC Tables 501.10(A)(1), (B)(1) (2026)","q":"In the 2026 NEC, permitted wiring methods for Class I locations are presented in:","o":["Chapter 9 Table 1","Annex C","Narrative text only","Tables, such as Table 501.10(A)(1)"],"a":3,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary and IAEI Magazine's proposal coverage (new tables in 501, 502, 503, 505, 506).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 547 Agricultural Buildings","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 equipotential plane construction: what changed?","b":"2023 NEC: an informational note referenced an ASABE standard but no construction details. 2026 NEC: 547.44(C) adds construction requirements (structural reinforcing steel, unencapsulated welded wire, or copper grid) with similar rules in Articles 555 and 682.","r":"NEC 547.44(C) (2026)","q":"Which is a recognized equipotential plane construction method in the 2026 547.44(C)?","o":["Aluminum foil","A single 6 AWG loop buried 6 in. deep","Isolated ground rods","Structural reinforcing steel, unencapsulated welded wire or a copper grid"],"a":3,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's FAQ and change summary and the Mike Holt course list (547.44, 555.14).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 555 Marinas","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 pier distribution systems: what may the AHJ require?","b":"2026 NEC: 555.9 lets the AHJ require documentation of an engineered design for a pier distribution system. Exception: one- and two-family dwellings with 240 V single-phase or less.","r":"NEC 555.9 (2026)","q":"Under 2026 555.9, the AHJ may request:","o":["Documentation of an engineered design for the pier distribution system","A GFCI test report only","A manufacturer warranty","A soil resistivity report"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article and Minnesota DLI's change summary.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 555 Marinas","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 GFPE for marinas: coordination and testing?","b":"2026 NEC: 555.35(F) requires ground-fault protection of equipment (GFPE) to be coordinated and performance tested by qualified persons per the manufacturer; written records must be available to the AHJ.","r":"NEC 555.35(F) (2026)","q":"Under 2026 555.35(F), GFPE for docking facility feeders must be:","o":["Set to trip at 30 mA","Coordinated and performance tested, with written records available to the AHJ","Installed without testing","Tested only by the manufacturer"],"a":1,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article and Minnesota DLI's change summary (555.35(F)).","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 625 EV Power Transfer","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026: who may install permanently installed EV chargers?","b":"2023 NEC: no qualification statement. 2026 NEC: 625.4 requires permanently installed EV power transfer equipment (Level 2 or higher) to be installed by qualified persons; NECA 413 is referenced as guidance.","r":"NEC 625.4 (2026)","q":"Under the new 2026 625.4, permanently installed EV power transfer equipment must be installed by:","o":["Qualified persons","Only utility employees","The vehicle manufacturer only","Anyone with a homeowner permit"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, LightNow, IAEI Magazine's EV article and Minnesota DLI.","d":2},{"ar":"Art. 625 EV Power Transfer","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 EVSE field markings?","b":"2026 NEC: 625.5 requires permanent visible markings on the EVSE enclosure for each incoming supply circuit (voltage, phases, frequency and full-load current) plus short-circuit current rating.","r":"NEC 625.5 (2026)","q":"Under 2026 625.5, EVSE enclosures must show for each supply circuit:","o":["Voltage, phases, frequency, full-load current, and short-circuit current rating","The charger brand","Only the voltage","The installer's license number"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, LightNow and IAEI Magazine.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 625 EV Power Transfer","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 EVSE emergency shutoff: distance and marking?","b":"2026 NEC: new 625.43(D) for other than one- and two-family dwellings requires a readily accessible manual-reset emergency shutoff within sight, 20 to 100 ft from the equipment, marked 'EVSE EMERGENCY DISCONNECT' with a warning that EVs remain energized.","r":"NEC 625.43(D) (2026)","q":"The 2026 EVSE emergency shutoff for non-dwelling installations must be located:","o":["Within sight and at least 20 ft but not more than 100 ft from the equipment","Within 5 ft of the charger","Not more than 10 ft","Inside the building only"],"a":0,"e":"Per EC&M's top-25 article, LightNow and IAEI Magazine.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 625 EV Power Transfer","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 GFCI/SPGFCI for permanently wired EVSE?","b":"2023 NEC: GFCI only for receptacles for EV charging. 2026 NEC: 625.54 adds GFCI and SPGFCI requirements for permanently wired equipment (details subject to NFPA TIA action).","r":"NEC 625.54 (2026)","q":"2026 625.54 extends ground-fault protection requirements to:","o":["Only receptacle outlets","Permanently wired EV charging equipment","Only residential garages","Only DC chargers"],"a":1,"e":"Per Minnesota DLI's change summary, IAEI Magazine and the Mike Holt course list. IAEI notes pending TIA actions on details, so verify current text.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 690 Solar PV","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 PV rounding rule?","b":"2023 NEC: no PV-specific rounding. 2026 NEC: 690.4(G) allows fractions of an ampere or volt below 0.5 to be dropped after correction and adjustment factors have been applied, then rounded.","r":"NEC 690.4(G) (2026)","q":"Under new 690.4(G), PV calculated currents and voltages may be rounded by:","o":["Truncating to the nearest 10","Rounding up always","Dropping fractions below 0.5 after applying correction factors","No rounding allowed"],"a":2,"e":"Per Solar Builder (Mayfield), Greenlancer and Portlandia Electric Supply.","d":3},{"ar":"Art. 702 Optional Standby","step":"2023->2026","f":"2026 702.4: new sizing option?","b":"2023 NEC: capacity options were full load or managed load. 2026 NEC: a third option for one- and two-family dwellings allows inverter-based systems using a listed power control system (UL 3141).","r":"NEC 702.4 (2026)","q":"The new third option in 2026 702.4 applies to:","o":["Marinas","One- and two-family dwellings using a listed power control system","All commercial buildings","Hospitals only"],"a":1,"e":"Per Solar Builder, Greenlancer, Minnesota DLI and Portlandia Electric Supply.","d":3}];